WN.lb(23,{"mt_9IzhGUZ30z":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"1 ten and {a} ones. What is the numeral?","answer":"10+a","hint":"Write 1 for the ten, then {a} for the ones.","explain":"1 ten and {a} ones makes {=10+a}.","params":{"a":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A teen word ends in -teen and its ones digit is {a}. What is the number?","answer":"10+a","hint":"Every teen number has 1 ten. Put 1 first, then the ones digit.","explain":"Teen numbers are 1 ten and some ones, so the number is {=10+a}.","params":{"a":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which numeral is nineteen?","choices":["19","91","90"],"answer":0,"hint":"You say nine first, but which digit shows the one ten?","explain":"Nineteen is 1 ten and 9 ones: 19. 91 puts the ones first, and 90 is ninety."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"How do you spell 15?","choices":["fifteen","fiveteen","fifty"],"answer":0,"hint":"Five changes its start in the teen word.","explain":"15 is fifteen: five changes to fif. Fifty ends in -ty and means 50."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which word is 6?","choices":["six","seven","sixteen"],"answer":0,"hint":"Read all the letters, not just the first one.","explain":"6 is six: s-i-x. Seven also starts with s but is 7, and sixteen is 16."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Mia hears 'fourteen' and writes 41. What went wrong?","choices":["She wrote the ones first","Nothing, 41 is right","She wrote forty"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which digit should show the one ten?","explain":"Fourteen is 1 ten and 4 ones, so 14. Mia wrote the digits in the order she heard them."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which word is 20?","choices":["twenty","twelve","twoteen"],"answer":0,"hint":"Words that end in -ty mean tens. 20 is 2 tens.","explain":"20 is twenty, 2 tens. Twelve is 12, and there is no twoteen."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"How do you spell 18?","choices":["eighteen","eightteen","eighty"],"answer":0,"hint":"Eight ends in t and teen starts with t. How many t letters?","explain":"18 is eighteen, with one t where eight and teen meet. Eighty is 80."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which number word does not end in -teen?","choices":["eleven","sixteen","thirteen"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the -teen ending.","explain":"Eleven has its own word with no teen. Sixteen and thirteen both end in -teen."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A teen number has 1 ten and {a} ones. Write it as a numeral.","answer":"10+a","hint":"Write the 1 for the ten first. Then write the ones.","explain":"One ten and {a} ones is {=10+a}. The 1 goes first even though you say the ones first.","params":{"a":[3,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Ten and {a} more. Write the numeral.","answer":"10+a","hint":"Ten and some more is a number from 11 to 20. Write 1 for the ten.","explain":"Ten and {a} more is {=10+a}: 1 ten and {a} ones.","params":{"a":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which numeral is seventeen?","choices":["17","71","70"],"answer":0,"hint":"Teen means one ten. Which digit shows the ten?","explain":"Seventeen is 1 ten and 7 ones, so 17. 71 writes the ones first, and 70 is seventy."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"How do you spell 13?","choices":["thirteen","threeteen","thirty"],"answer":0,"hint":"Three changes its start in the teen word. The word ends in -teen.","explain":"13 is thirteen: three changes to thir. Thirty ends in -ty and means 30."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which word is 12?","choices":["twelve","twoteen","twenty"],"answer":0,"hint":"11 and 12 have their own words with no teen.","explain":"12 is twelve. There is no twoteen, and twenty is 20."}],"explain":{"kid":["A numeral is a number written with digits, like 6. A number word is the same number in letters, like six. Read every letter. Six and seven both start with s.","Teen words are tricky. In seventeen you say seven first. But you write the ten first. Teen means one ten. So seventeen is 1 ten and 7 ones: 17.","Some teen words change their start. Three turns into thir, so 13 is thirteen. Five turns into fif, so 15 is fifteen. Eight and teen share a t, so 18 is eighteen.","Eleven and twelve have their own words, with no teen. Twenty means 2 tens. Words that end in -ty are tens, so thirty is 30 and not 13."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 6, Year 1. The National Curriculum asks children to read and write numbers from 1 to 20 in numerals and words. Key words: numeral, digit, number word, teen number, tens, ones. The teen words are the hard part, because English says the ones before the ten.","The most common mistake is writing the digits in the order they are heard: seventeen becomes 71. Show a teen number as one full ten frame and some more, and say it as one ten and seven, seventeen. Children also mix -teen and -ty (thirteen and thirty), so stress the ending when you say them.","Spelling slips are predictable: threeteen, fiveteen, eightteen and twoteen. Point out that three changes to thir, five to fif, eight and teen share one t, and that 11 and 12 have their own words. Children who read only the first letter confuse six with seven, so ask them to read each word letter by letter.","In the lab your child reads three words and taps the numerals, builds 17, 14 and 19 in a tens box and a ones box, picks the right spelling for 13, 15, 18 and 12, drags word cards onto an 11 to 20 track, and checks a robot that writes 61 for sixteen and 20 for twelve."]},"familyTip":"Write the number words one to twenty on cards and the numerals on sticky notes. Turn them all face down and play pairs. When your child turns over a teen word, ask them to say which digit they write first and why. Spot number words on signs, birthday cards and book pages.","keyIdea":"Every numeral from 1 to 20 has a number word. Read all the letters, because six and seven both start with s. Teen words say the ones first, but you write the ten first: seventeen is 17, not 71. Eleven, twelve and twenty have no teen in them, and words that end in -ty mean tens.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Teen number or not? Drag each word, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Teen number (13 to 19)","Not a teen number"],"items":[["thirteen",0],["twelve",1],["nineteen",0],["twenty",1],["fifteen",0],["eleven",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagnumword","caption":"Flagship lab: read words to numerals, build teen numbers in tens and ones boxes, pick teen spellings, drag words onto a track, spot a robot's slips.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_hCVPYlF-7Y":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A square has {n} tiles on each side. How many tiles?","answer":"n*n","hint":"The tiles make {n} rows of {n}, so work out {n} × {n}.","explain":"{n} rows of {n} tiles is {n} × {n} = {=n*n} tiles.","params":{"n":[3,10]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A cube is {n} blocks long, wide and tall. How many blocks?","answer":"n*n*n","hint":"Multiply length × width × height: {n} × {n} × {n}.","explain":"{n} × {n} × {n} = {=n*n*n} blocks, which is {n} cubed.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which number is both a square and a cube?","choices":["64","16","27"],"answer":0,"hint":"Try 8 × 8 and then 4 × 4 × 4.","explain":"64 = 8 × 8 and 64 = 4 × 4 × 4, so it is both a square and a cube number."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"What is {n}² − {m}²?","answer":"n*n-m*m","hint":"Square each first.","explain":"{=n*n} − {=m*m} = {=n*n-m*m}.","params":{"n":[6,10],"m":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What is 1³?","choices":["1","3","0"],"answer":0,"hint":"Work out 1 × 1 × 1.","explain":"1 × 1 × 1 = 1, so 1 cubed is 1."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which is a cube number?","choices":["125","100","50"],"answer":0,"hint":"A cube number is a number times itself three times. Try 5 × 5 × 5.","explain":"125 is a cube number because 5 × 5 × 5 = 125."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"The square root of {=n*n} is…","answer":"n","hint":"Which number squared?","explain":"{n} × {n} = {=n*n}.","params":{"n":[2,12]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"What is {n}²?","answer":"n*n","hint":"Squaring means multiplying a number by itself, so work out {n} × {n}.","explain":"{n} squared means {n} × {n}, which is {=n*n}.","params":{"n":[2,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"What is {n}³?","answer":"n*n*n","hint":"n × n × n.","explain":"{n} × {n} × {n} = {=n*n*n}.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Is 3² the same as 3 × 2?","choices":["No, 3² = 9","Yes, both are 6","Yes, both are 9"],"answer":0,"hint":"The small 2 means multiply 3 by itself, not multiply by 2.","explain":"3² = 3 × 3 = 9, but 3 × 2 = 6, so they are not the same."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which is a square number?","choices":["36","20","12"],"answer":0,"hint":"A square number is a number times itself. Try 6 × 6.","explain":"36 is a square number because 6 × 6 = 36."}],"explain":{"kid":["Make a square with 3 rows of 3 tiles. That is 9 tiles. So 9 is a square number. We write 3² = 9.","Stack 3 layers of 3 by 3 cubes. That is 27 cubes, so 27 is a cube number: 3³ = 27."],"adult":["n² (n squared) means n × n; the name comes from the square of tiles. n³ (n cubed) means n × n × n; the name comes from a cube of blocks.","The first square numbers are 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81, 100. The first cubes are 1, 8, 27, 64, 125. 64 is both: 8² and 4³. A common mistake is 3² = 6: squaring is not doubling."]},"familyTip":"Build squares from coins: 1, 4, 9, 16. Do the gaps between them make a pattern?","keyIdea":"A square number is n × n (written n²); a cube number is n × n × n (written n³).","visual":{"type":"squares","caption":"Change n. Count the tiles in the square and the little cubes in the stack.","opts":{"n":3}}},"mt_76SPWvdI7r":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Write the number that is {a} tens and 0 ones.","answer":"10*a","params":{"a":[2,9]},"hint":"No ones means the ones digit is 0.","explain":"{a} tens and no ones is {=10*a}. Its word ends in -ty and has no ones word."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"How many ones are in {=10*a+b}?","answer":"b","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,9]},"hint":"The last digit tells you the ones.","explain":"{=10*a+b} is {a} tens and {b} ones, so it has {b} ones. That is the word after the hyphen."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which is bigger: nineteen or ninety?","choices":["ninety","nineteen","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is a tens word, ending in -ty?","explain":"Ninety is 90 and nineteen is 19, so ninety is bigger."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which word is spelled correctly?","choices":["fifty","fivety","fiveteen"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say it out loud: the five changes to fif.","explain":"Fifty is the right spelling for 50. Fivety and fiveteen are not words."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which shows sixty-four written the right way?","choices":["sixty-four","sixty four","sixtyfour"],"answer":0,"hint":"A short line joins the tens word and the ones word.","explain":"We write a hyphen between the tens word and the ones word: sixty-four."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What number is thirty-eight?","choices":["38","83","308"],"answer":0,"hint":"Thirty is 3 tens. Eight is 8 ones.","explain":"3 tens and 8 ones is 38. 83 swaps the digits and 308 has a hundreds digit."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What is ten tens?","choices":["one hundred","ten","one thousand"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count in tens: ten, twenty, thirty … ninety, and one more ten.","explain":"Ten tens make one hundred, written 100."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which list goes from smallest to biggest?","choices":["fourteen, forty, forty-one","forty, fourteen, forty-one","forty-one, forty, fourteen"],"answer":0,"hint":"Turn each word into a number first.","explain":"Fourteen is 14, forty is 40 and forty-one is 41, so the order is 14, 40, 41."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A number has {a} tens and {b} ones. What is the number?","answer":"10*a+b","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,9]},"hint":"The tens digit goes first, then the ones digit.","explain":"{a} tens and {b} ones make {=10*a+b}. Say it as the tens word, then the ones word."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"How many tens are in {=10*a+b}?","answer":"a","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,9]},"hint":"Look at the first digit. It tells you the tens.","explain":"{=10*a+b} is {a} tens and {b} ones, so it has {a} tens. The tens word comes first when you say it."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which word is 40?","choices":["forty","fourteen","fourty"],"answer":0,"hint":"Whole tens end in -ty. Check the spelling too.","explain":"40 is four tens: forty. Fourteen is 14, and forty has no u."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"How do you write seventy-six in numerals?","choices":["76","706","67"],"answer":0,"hint":"Seventy is 7 tens. Six is 6 ones.","explain":"7 tens and 6 ones is 76. 706 is seven hundred and six, and 67 swaps the digits."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which is 92 written in words?","choices":["ninety-two","ninty-two","twenty-nine"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say the tens first: 9 tens. Then the ones.","explain":"92 is 9 tens and 2 ones: ninety-two. Ninety keeps the e, and twenty-nine is 29."}],"explain":{"kid":["Big numbers have two parts. 57 is 5 tens and 7 ones. We say the tens part first: fifty. Then the ones part: seven.","We write it the same way: fifty-seven. The short line in the middle is a hyphen. It joins the two parts.","Tens words end in ty. Twenty is 2 tens. Forty is 4 tens. Teen words are not tens. Fourteen is just 14.","Forty has no u. We say fifty, not fivety. Seventy is 7 tens and no ones, so it has no ones word. Ten tens make one hundred."],"adult":["Ages 6 to 7. England's Year 2 maths asks children to read and write numbers to at least 100 in numerals and in words, and to know the place value of each digit (tens and ones). Key words: tens, ones, digit, numeral, number word, hyphen.","Every number from 21 to 99 that is not a whole ten is written as a tens word, a hyphen and a ones word, so the word maps straight onto the two digits: eighty-three is 8 tens and 3 ones, 83. Whole tens end in -ty, and 100 is one hundred.","Common mistakes: confusing teen and tens words (fourteen for 40, seventeen for 70), the spellings fourty, fivety and ninty, writing sixty-two as 602 because sixty is written 60 first, swapping the digits (26 for sixty-two) and leaving out the hyphen. Ask your child to say how many tens the word has before they write the numeral.","In the lab your child picks the tens word for rods of ten, joins a tens word and a ones word to label a number, builds a word from rods and cubes, puts words in order of size, and checks a robot that writes fourty and eighteen for 80."]},"familyTip":"Write a number from 21 to 99 on a sticky note and ask your child to write it in words, saying the tens word first. Then swap: you write a word such as forty-six and they write the numeral. Use pasta in cups of ten for the tens and single pieces for the ones. Ask: is fourteen or forty the bigger number?","keyIdea":"A two-digit number is said and written as a tens word, a hyphen, then a ones word: 57 is fifty-seven, 5 tens and 7 ones. Words for whole tens end in -ty (twenty, thirty, forty, fifty … ninety); words ending in -teen are 13 to 19. Watch the spellings forty (no u), fifty and ninety. A tens number such as seventy has no ones word, and ten tens make one hundred.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it less than 50, or 50 or more? Drag each word, or tap it and then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Less than 50","50 or more"],"items":[["forty-nine",0],["fifty-one",1],["fourteen",0],["ninety",1],["thirty-five",0],["sixty-three",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagword100","caption":"Flagship lab: pick tens words for rods, join tens and ones words with a hyphen, build words from rods and cubes, order words, check a robot.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_To9HdLy8vq":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"What number is D followed by {a} Cs and then {b} Is (for example DCCI)?","answer":"500+100*a+b","params":{"a":[1,3],"b":[1,3]},"hint":"D is 500. Each C after it adds 100 and each I adds 1.","explain":"D is 500, {a} Cs add {=100*a} and {b} Is add {b}, so the value is {=500+100*a+b}."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A year starts MM and ends with tens and ones worth {=10*a+b}. It has no hundreds. What is the year?","answer":"2000+10*a+b","params":{"a":[1,9],"b":[0,9]},"hint":"MM is 2000. Add the tens and ones.","explain":"MM is 2000, there are no hundreds, and the tens and ones add {=10*a+b}, so the year is {=2000+10*a+b}."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What number is CM?","choices":["900","1100","100"],"answer":0,"hint":"C is before M, so it is taken away.","explain":"C before M takes away: 1000 − 100 = 900. 1100 would be MC."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"How is 999 written in Roman numerals?","choices":["CMXCIX","IM","CMIC"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do one place at a time: 900, then 90, then 9.","explain":"900 is CM, 90 is XC and 9 is IX, so 999 is CMXCIX. I can only go before V or X."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What year is MMXXIV?","choices":["2024","2026","20024"],"answer":0,"hint":"Split it: MM, then XX, then IV. Add the chunks.","explain":"MM is 2000, XX is 20 and IV is 4: 2024. 2026 reads IV as 6."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Where should you split MCDLXXXV to read it?","choices":["M | CD | LXXX | V","MC | DL | XXX | V","M | C | DLXXXV"],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep take-away pairs such as CD together.","explain":"The chunks are M (1000), CD (400), LXXX (80) and V (5), so MCDLXXXV is 1485."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"How is 1990 written in Roman numerals?","choices":["MCMXC","MXM","MDCCCCXC"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write 1000, then 900, then 90, one place at a time.","explain":"1000 is M, 900 is CM and 90 is XC, so 1990 is MCMXC. XM is not an allowed pair."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"What number is D?","choices":["500","50","1000"],"answer":0,"hint":"L is 50 and M is 1000. D sits between them.","explain":"D stands for 500. It is half of M, which is 1000."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"What number is M followed by {a} Cs and then {b} Xs (for example MCCX)?","answer":"1000+100*a+10*b","params":{"a":[1,3],"b":[1,3]},"hint":"A smaller letter after a bigger one is added.","explain":"M is 1000, each C adds 100 and each X adds 10: 1000 + {=100*a} + {=10*b} = {=1000+100*a+10*b}."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"The thousands chunk of a year is M and the hundreds chunk is CM. The last two digits are {a}{b}. What is the year?","answer":"1900+10*a+b","params":{"a":[1,9],"b":[1,9]},"hint":"M is 1000 and CM is 900. Then add the tens and ones.","explain":"M + CM is 1000 + 900 = 1900. Adding {=10*a+b} gives {=1900+10*a+b}."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What number is MCMXCIX?","choices":["1999","2221","2199"],"answer":0,"hint":"Split it into chunks: M, CM, XC, IX.","explain":"M is 1000, CM is 900, XC is 90 and IX is 9, so MCMXCIX is 1999. 2221 adds every letter."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"How is 400 written in Roman numerals?","choices":["CD","CCCC","DC"],"answer":0,"hint":"Never four of the same letter in a row. Take away from D.","explain":"400 is 500 take away 100, written CD. CCCC breaks the three-in-a-row rule and DC is 600."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which is the bigger number: DCCCLXXXVIII or M?","choices":["M","DCCCLXXXVIII","They are equal"],"answer":0,"hint":"Work out each value. Do not count the letters.","explain":"DCCCLXXXVIII is 500 + 300 + 50 + 30 + 5 + 3 = 888, and M is 1000, so M is bigger."}],"explain":{"kid":["You already know I, V, X, L and C. Two more letters take you to 1000. D is 500 and M is 1000.","A smaller letter before a bigger one is taken away. CD is 500 take away 100, which is 400. CM is 1000 take away 100, which is 900. Only six pairs work this way: IV, IX, XL, XC, CD and CM.","Long numerals are easy if you cut them into chunks: thousands, then hundreds, then tens, then ones. MCMXCIX is M, CM, XC and IX. That is 1000 + 900 + 90 + 9, so it is 1999.","To write a year, do one place at a time. 2024 is MM for 2000, nothing for the hundreds, XX for 20 and IV for 4: MMXXIV. Count values, not letters. DCCCLXXXVIII is long, but it is only 888, less than M."],"adult":["Ages 9 to 10. Year 5 of the National Curriculum in England asks pupils to read Roman numerals to 1000 (M) and to spot years written in them. Year 4 went up to C (100). The new symbols are D (500) and M (1000), and the new take-away pairs are CD (400) and CM (900).","The rules: write symbols from largest to smallest and add them. A smaller symbol just before a larger one is taken away, but only in IV, IX, XL, XC, CD and CM. No symbol goes more than three times in a row, and V, L and D are never doubled. Each digit of our number maps to one Roman chunk, so 1485 is M, CD, LXXX, V.","Common mistakes: adding every symbol (MCMXCIX read as 2221), writing CCCC for 400, and making up pairs such as IM for 999, XM for 990 or MIM for 1999. Children also split a pair across two chunks, treat the letters like digits, or think a longer numeral is bigger. Ask your child to draw lines between the chunks before adding.","In the lab your child builds 300 to 1000 from C, D and M tiles, taps the gaps that split a numeral into chunks, and sets four columns to write years. They also pick the bigger of two numerals when the longer one is smaller, and check a robot that says IM is 999."]},"familyTip":"Look for years in Roman numerals at the end of film credits, on foundation stones and on old clocks and coins. Read one together by drawing lines between the thousands, hundreds, tens and ones. Then write family birth years in Roman numerals and check each other's work.","keyIdea":"Two more symbols take Roman numerals past 100: D is 500 and M is 1000. The take-away rule now has two more pairs: CD is 400 and CM is 900. To read a long numeral, split it into thousands, hundreds, tens and ones, then add the chunks: MCMXCIX is M + CM + XC + IX = 1000 + 900 + 90 + 9 = 1999. To write a year, do one place at a time. A longer numeral is not always a bigger number.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Less than 500, or 500 or more? Drag each numeral, or tap it and then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Less than 500","500 or more"],"items":[["CD",0],["DC",1],["CDXCIX",0],["DI",1],["CCCXC",0],["M",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagromanm","caption":"Flagship lab: make hundreds from C, D and M, split long numerals into place-value chunks, dial a year column by column, compare numerals, check a robot.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_HLUqHJ9Y7n":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"What is {a} × 10²?","answer":"a*100","params":{"a":[11,95]},"hint":"10² is 100. Move every digit 2 places left.","explain":"{a} × 10² = {a} × 100 = {=a*100}. The digits moved 2 places left and two zeros hold the tens and ones places."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"{a} × 10ⁿ = {a}0000. What is n?","answer":"4","params":{"a":[2,9]},"hint":"Count how many places the {a} moved to the left.","explain":"The {a} moved from the ones place to the ten-thousands place, which is 4 places, so n = 4 and 10⁴ = 10,000."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What is 56 ÷ 10²?","choices":["0.56","5600","5.6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Dividing makes it smaller. Move every digit 2 places right.","explain":"The 5 moves from tens to tenths and the 6 from ones to hundredths: 0.56. 5600 moved the wrong way, and 5.6 moved only 1 place."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"2.56 × 10ⁿ = 2560. What is n?","choices":["3","1","2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count how far the 2 moved, not how many zeros are in 2560.","explain":"The 2 moved from ones to thousands, which is 3 places, so n = 3. 2560 has only one zero because 5 and 6 fill two of the places."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What is 7 ÷ 10³?","choices":["0.007","0.07","7000"],"answer":0,"hint":"10³ is 1000. Move the 7 three places right.","explain":"The 7 moves from ones to thousandths: 0.007. Zeros hold the tenths and hundredths places."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which is the same as 10 × 10 × 10 × 10 × 10?","choices":["10⁵","10 × 5","5¹⁰"],"answer":0,"hint":"The exponent counts how many tens are multiplied.","explain":"Five tens multiplied together is 10⁵ = 100,000. 10 × 5 is only 50."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What is 0.3 × 10³?","choices":["300","0.3000","30"],"answer":0,"hint":"Move the 3 three places left, starting from the tenths.","explain":"The 3 moves from tenths to ones, tens, then hundreds: 300. Adding zeros after a decimal, as in 0.3000, does not change its value."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A city has about 2 × 10⁶ people. About how many is that?","choices":["2,000,000","200,000","12"],"answer":0,"hint":"10⁶ is a 1 followed by 6 zeros.","explain":"10⁶ is 1,000,000, so 2 × 10⁶ is 2,000,000, two million."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"What is {a} × 10³?","answer":"a*1000","params":{"a":[2,99]},"hint":"10³ is 1000. Every digit moves 3 places left.","explain":"{a} × 10³ = {a} × 1000 = {=a*1000}. Each digit moved 3 places left and zeros filled the empty places."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"How many zeros are there after the 1 when 10 to the power {a} is written out in full?","answer":"a","params":{"a":[2,9]},"hint":"Each 10 you multiply by moves the 1 one more place left.","explain":"10 to the power {a} is {a} tens multiplied together, so the 1 is followed by {a} zeros."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"What is {a}00 ÷ 10²?","answer":"a","params":{"a":[3,9]},"hint":"10² is 100. Dividing moves every digit 2 places right.","explain":"{a}00 ÷ 10² = {a}00 ÷ 100 = {a}. The {a} moved from the hundreds place to the ones place."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"What is 3.4 × 10²?","choices":["340","3.400","34"],"answer":0,"hint":"Move every digit 2 places left. The decimal point stays.","explain":"The 3 moves from ones to hundreds and the 4 from tenths to tens: 340. Writing 3.400 adds zeros but leaves the value at 3.4."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"What is 10⁴?","choices":["10,000","40","104"],"answer":0,"hint":"The 4 counts how many tens are multiplied together.","explain":"10⁴ = 10 × 10 × 10 × 10 = 10,000: a 1 followed by 4 zeros. It is not 10 × 4."}],"explain":{"kid":["A power of ten is tens multiplied together. We write 10 × 10 × 10 as 10³. The small 3 is the exponent. It counts the tens. So 10³ is 1000, not 30.","Each time you multiply by 10, the 1 moves one place left and a zero fills the gap. So 10² is 100 and 10⁶ is 1,000,000. The exponent tells you how many zeros come after the 1.","When you multiply any number by 10ⁿ, every digit moves n places left. The decimal point stays still. So 3.4 × 10² = 340. Putting two zeros on the end would give 3.400, which is still 3.4.","When you divide by 10ⁿ, every digit moves n places right. So 56 ÷ 10² = 0.56. To find a power, count the places the digits moved. 2.56 × 10³ = 2560 has one zero, but the digits moved 3 places."],"adult":["Ages 10 to 11. This meets Common Core Grade 5 standard 5.NBT.A.2: explain patterns in the number of zeros and in the place of the decimal point when multiplying or dividing by a power of 10, and use whole-number exponents for powers of 10. In England, Year 5 asks pupils to multiply and divide whole numbers and decimals by 10, 100 and 1000, and to use the notation for squared and cubed.","The reason is place value: each place is worth ten times the place to its right. So × 10 moves every digit one place left and × 10ⁿ moves it n places. Teachers often say 'the digits move, the point stays'. That is more accurate than 'move the decimal point', and it works for whole numbers and decimals alike. Zeros fill empty places up to the ones, and a zero goes in the ones place for numbers below 1.","Common mistakes: reading 10³ as 10 × 3 = 30 or as 103, and adding zeros to a decimal (3.4 × 100 = 3.400). Children also move the digits the wrong way when dividing, or find the power by counting zeros in the answer. That only works when you start from a whole number with no zeros on the end. Ask your child how many places each digit moved.","In the lab your child builds 10², 10⁴ and 10⁶ by tapping × 10 and watching the 1 travel. They drag the digits of 3.4, 47, 2.56 and 0.8 left to multiply, and of 560, 7, 45.2 and 1200 right to divide. Then they find the power that links two numbers and check a robot's facts."]},"familyTip":"Write a number such as 4.7 on a strip of paper, one digit per square, and draw a place-value chart with a fixed decimal point. Slide the strip left to multiply by 10, 100 or 1000 and right to divide, saying the power of ten each time. Then spot big numbers in the news, like a city of 2 million, and write them with a power of ten.","keyIdea":"A power of ten is tens multiplied together. The small raised number, the exponent, counts the tens: 10³ = 10 × 10 × 10 = 1000, so 10³ is a 1 followed by 3 zeros. Multiplying by 10ⁿ moves every digit n places to the left, and dividing by 10ⁿ moves every digit n places to the right. The decimal point stays where it is: 3.4 × 10² = 340 and 56 ÷ 10² = 0.56. Count the places moved, not the zeros: 2.56 × 10³ = 2560 has only one zero.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Equal to 1000, or not? Drag each card, or tap it and then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Equal to 1000","Not 1000"],"items":[["10³",0],["10 × 3",1],["10 × 10 × 10",0],["1 × 10²",1],["0.1 × 10⁴",0],["10,000 ÷ 10",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagpow10","caption":"Flagship lab: build 10² to 10⁶ from tens, slide digits left and right across a place-value chart, find the power that links two numbers, check a robot.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_r0VXbfAmsH":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"How many ones are the same as {t} tens?","answer":"t*10","hint":"Each ten is 10 ones. Count in tens {t} times.","explain":"{t} tens is {t} lots of 10, which is {=t*10} ones.","params":{"t":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"What number is 1 ten and {o} ones?","answer":"10+o","hint":"Start at 10 for the ten. Count on {o} more.","explain":"1 ten is 10. Count on {o} ones to get {=10+o}.","params":{"o":[0,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Mia sees 2 ten sticks and 4 cubes. She says it is 6. What went wrong?","choices":["She counted each ten stick as 1","Nothing went wrong","She forgot the 4 cubes"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many cubes are in one ten stick?","explain":"Each stick is 10 cubes, so 2 sticks is 20. With 4 more it is 24, not 6."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"How do you write 1 ten and 7 ones?","choices":["17","71","8"],"answer":0,"hint":"The tens digit goes on the left, then the ones digit.","explain":"Tens go first, so 1 ten and 7 ones is written 17. 71 would be 7 tens and 1 one."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"You swap 10 loose ones for 1 ten. What happens to how many cubes you have?","choices":["It stays the same","It gets 10 fewer","It gets 10 more"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did any cubes go in the bin, or did they just get snapped together?","explain":"Swapping only groups the cubes. 10 ones and 1 ten are the same amount, so the total stays the same."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What does the 0 in 30 tell you?","choices":["There are no ones left over","There is nothing at all","There are 0 tens"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which place is the 0 in: the tens or the ones?","explain":"30 is 3 tens and 0 ones. The 0 holds the ones place and says there are no extra ones."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"How many tens are in 46?","choices":["4","6","46"],"answer":0,"hint":"The left digit counts the tens.","explain":"46 is 4 tens and 6 ones, so there are 4 tens. 46 is the whole number."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which is worth exactly 10?","choices":["5 ones and 5 ones","9 ones","1 ten and 1 one"],"answer":0,"hint":"Add up the ones in each card. Which makes 10?","explain":"5 ones and 5 ones is 10 ones, which is 1 ten. 9 ones is one short and 1 ten and 1 one is 11."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"How many ones are in 1 ten?","choices":["10","1","100"],"answer":0,"hint":"Picture a ten stick. Count the cubes in it one by one.","explain":"A ten is 10 ones stuck together, so 1 ten is the same as 10 ones."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"{t} tens and {o} ones. What number is that?","answer":"t*10+o","hint":"Count the tens in tens: 10, 20, 30 and so on. Then count on {o} ones.","explain":"{t} tens is {=t*10}. Count on {o} ones to get {=t*10+o}.","params":{"t":[1,9],"o":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"You have {n} loose cubes. You snap 10 into a ten stick. How many loose cubes are left?","answer":"n-10","hint":"The ten stick uses 10 cubes. Take 10 away from {n}.","explain":"{n} is 1 ten and {=n-10} ones, so {=n-10} cubes are still loose.","params":{"n":[11,19]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which is more: 2 tens or 15 ones?","choices":["2 tens","15 ones","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Turn the tens into ones. How many ones are in 2 tens?","explain":"2 tens is 20 ones, and 20 is more than 15. More bits does not mean more."}],"explain":{"kid":["Count out 10 cubes. Snap them into one tall stick. Now it is one ten. It is still 10 cubes. We just put them in a bundle.","Look at 13. The 1 is for one ten. The 3 is for three ones. So 13 is a ten stick and 3 loose cubes.","Got lots of loose ones? Count 10 of them and snap them into a stick. You swap 10 ones for 1 ten. You still have just as many cubes.","A ten stick is one thing, but it is worth 10. So 1 ten is more than 9 ones, even though 9 ones are more bits."],"adult":["This is the start of place value. US Common Core 1.NBT.B.2a says first graders should understand that 10 can be thought of as a bundle of ten ones, called a ten, and 1.NBT.B.2b and 2c extend that to the teen numbers (a ten and some ones) and the decades (some tens and 0 ones). In England, the Year 1 and Year 2 programmes of study ask children to count in tens and to recognise the place value of each digit in a two-digit number.","The big step is unitising: seeing ten things as one unit while still knowing it is ten. Base-ten blocks (a rod of 10 and single unit cubes), bundles of straws and a tens-and-ones chart all show it. Exchanging, sometimes called regrouping, is swapping 10 ones for 1 ten or back again; the amount never changes, only how it is grouped. That idea later carries all of column addition and subtraction.","Common mistakes to watch for: counting a ten rod as 1, so 2 rods and 4 cubes becomes 6; writing the digits the wrong way round, so 1 ten and 7 ones becomes 71; thinking that more pieces means more, so 15 ones looks bigger than 2 tens; thinking that cubes vanish in a swap; and reading the 0 in 10 or 30 as nothing at all rather than as a zero that holds the ones place and says no extra ones.","In the lab your child bundles 10 loose cubes into a ten, reads three numbers built from rods and cubes, circles 10 ones to swap them for a ten and sees the total stay the same, builds 17, 30 and 10 on a tens-and-ones chart (10 ones swap themselves for a ten), and compares a ten with piles of ones."]},"familyTip":"Bundle drinking straws or lolly sticks into tens with a hair band, and keep the rest loose. Grab a handful, bundle what you can, then say the number: 2 bundles and 6 loose is 26. Ask: did we lose any straws when we bundled them?","keyIdea":"Ten ones can be bundled into one group called a ten. A ten rod is 10 cubes stuck together, so 1 ten and 10 ones are the same amount. In a two-digit number the left digit counts tens and the right digit counts ones: 13 is 1 ten and 3 ones, and the 0 in 10 means no ones are left over. Swapping 10 ones for a ten never changes how many there are.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it worth exactly 10? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Worth 10","Not 10"],"items":[["10 ones",0],["1 ten",0],["9 ones",1],["1 ten and 1 one",1],["5 ones and 5 ones",0],["2 tens",1],["1 ten and 0 ones",0],["100 ones",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagbundle","caption":"Flagship lab: bundle 10 ones into a ten, read tens and ones, swap 10 ones for a ten, build numbers on a chart and compare a ten with ones.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_xmmgAzxe5j":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which number is a teen?","choices":["13","30","3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Teen numbers are the numbers from 11 up to 19 only.","explain":"13 is between 11 and 19. 30 is three tens. 3 is just ones."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"19 = 10 + ?","choices":["9","19","1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pull out the ten; count what remains.","explain":"19 is one ten and nine ones, so 19 = 10 + 9."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Why is 11 a teen number?","choices":["it is 10 + 1","it looks like two 1s side by side","it is bigger than 20"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of eleven as one full ten and one leftover one.","explain":"11 is one ten and one one. Looking like two 1s is about digits, not value."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"10 + 0 is…","choices":["10, not a teen","10, a teen","0"],"answer":0,"hint":"Teens need leftover ones from 1 to 9.","explain":"10 is one ten and zero ones. Teens start at 11."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Sam counts only the four leftover ones and says the number is 4. The ten-frame is full. What did Sam miss?","choices":["the full ten, so it is 14","nothing, it is 4","it must be 40"],"answer":0,"hint":"A full ten-frame is worth ten, not one.","explain":"The full frame is one ten. Ten and four is fourteen."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which shows 16?","choices":["1 ten and 6 ones","6 tens","16 tens"],"answer":0,"hint":"A teen has exactly one ten, then some leftover ones beside it.","explain":"16 = 10 + 6. Six tens would be 60."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"One ten and {n} ones. What total?","answer":"10+n","hint":"Start from ten, then count on the leftover ones to get the total.","explain":"One ten and {n} ones make the teen {=10+n}.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"You have {n}. Pull out one ten. How many ones remain?","answer":"n-10","hint":"Subtract 10 from the teen.","explain":"After you pull one ten out of {n}, {=n-10} ones remain.","params":{"n":[12,18]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"14 is the same as…","choices":["10 + 4","1 + 4","40 + 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep one full ten, then count the leftover ones.","explain":"Fourteen is one ten and four ones, written 10 + 4."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A teen number always has…","choices":["one full ten and some ones","two full tens","no tens at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"Teens sit between 11 and 19.","explain":"Every teen has exactly one ten plus some leftover ones."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"10 + 7 =","choices":["17","7","70"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start at ten, then count on seven ones.","explain":"Ten and seven ones make seventeen."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"One ten and {n} ones make what number?","answer":"10+n","hint":"Add the leftover ones on to the full ten to find the teen total.","explain":"One ten plus {n} ones makes the teen number {=10+n}.","params":{"n":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"{n} is a teen. How many ones are left after you pull out one ten?","answer":"n-10","hint":"Take 10 away from the teen number.","explain":"{n} − 10 = {=n-10} leftover ones.","params":{"n":[11,19]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Teen numbers go from 11 to 19. Each one is a full ten and some more ones.","Fill a ten-frame. That is one ten. Then put leftover ones beside it. Ten and four is fourteen.","You can also split a teen. Start with 14 ones, pull out a ten, and count 4 left. So 14 = 10 + 4.","The first digit in 14 means one ten. The second digit means four ones."],"adult":["Kindergarten and Year 1 place value ask children to compose and decompose numbers from 11 to 19 into ten ones and some further ones (CCSS K.NBT.1, 1.NBT.2.b; early NC place value). Keep the language concrete: ten-frame, leftover ones, 10 + n.","Words to use: teen, ten-frame, ten, ones, leftover, compose, decompose, 10 + 4 = 14. Common mistakes: counting only the leftover ones and saying 14 is 4; reading digits backwards (41); thinking 11 is two separate ones with no ten; calling 10 itself a teen.","In the lab your child fills a ten-frame, adds leftover ones, reads teens as 10 + n, splits 14 into ten and ones, and builds 16."]},"familyTip":"Use an egg box cut to ten holes, or draw a ten-frame on paper. Make 14 with ten in the frame and four beside it. Say 'ten and four' before you say 'fourteen'.","keyIdea":"Teen numbers from 11 to 19 are one full ten and some leftover ones. Fourteen is a ten-frame filled plus four more: 10 + 4. Seventeen is 10 + 7. Seeing teens this way is early place value: the 1 in 14 stands for one ten, and the 4 stands for four ones.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Teen or not? Drag each number, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Teen (11-19)","Not a teen"],"items":[["14",0],["10",1],["17",0],["20",1],["11",0],["9",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagteens","caption":"Flagship lab: fill a ten-frame, add leftover ones, read teens as 10+n, split a teen, and build 16.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_fR0UtsSREU":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"To write sixteen, tap…","choices":["1 then 6","6 then 1","6 then 6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Tens digit first, then ones.","explain":"Sixteen is 16: digit 1 then digit 6."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"20 means…","choices":["2 tens and 0 ones","2 ones","20 tens"],"answer":0,"hint":"The left digit counts tens.","explain":"20 is two tens and zero ones — twenty."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"19 is…","choices":["nineteen","ninety-one","nine"],"answer":0,"hint":"Read tens, then ones. Do not swap.","explain":"19 is nineteen. 91 would be ninety-one."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Why are 12 and 21 different?","choices":["the digits sit in different places","they use different digits","they are the same number"],"answer":0,"hint":"Place value: left is tens, right is ones.","explain":"Same digits, different places: 12 is twelve, 21 is twenty-one."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Bead on a 0–20 line at ten and {n} ones. Where is it?","answer":"10+n","hint":"Find ten on the line, then count on {n} more ones to the right.","explain":"10 + {n} = {=10+n} on the line.","params":{"n":[0,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"You count {n} objects. Which numeral do you write?","answer":"n","hint":"The numeral is the same count you just made.","explain":"Counting {n} objects matches the numeral {n}.","params":{"n":[4,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which is a teen number?","choices":["15","20","8"],"answer":0,"hint":"Teen numbers are the numbers from eleven to nineteen only.","explain":"15 is fifteen, a teen. 20 is twenty; 8 is a single digit."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Left digit of 18 is the…","choices":["tens","ones","hundreds"],"answer":0,"hint":"In a two-digit number the left place is tens.","explain":"In 18 the 1 means 1 ten; the 8 means 8 ones."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which numeral matches seven dots?","choices":["7","17","70"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the dots, then find the mark that means that count — not a teen or a ten times bigger.","explain":"Seven dots match the numeral 7. 17 is seventeen; 70 is seventy."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"14 is…","choices":["1 ten and 4 ones","4 tens and 1 one","14 tens"],"answer":0,"hint":"Teen numbers have one ten. The other digit tells the ones.","explain":"14 = 10 + 4, so 1 ten and 4 ones."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A ten-rod and {n} ones make which number?","answer":"10+n","hint":"Start at 10, then add the ones.","explain":"10 + {n} = {=10+n}. That is a teen or twenty when {n} is 10.","params":{"n":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"The numeral shows 1 ten and {n} ones. What number is it?","answer":"10+n","hint":"Tens digit 1, ones digit {n}. Put them together as a two-digit number.","explain":"1 ten and {n} ones is the number {=10+n}.","params":{"n":[0,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"The word twelve matches…","choices":["12","21","20"],"answer":0,"hint":"Twelve is one ten and two ones — digits 1 then 2.","explain":"Twelve is 12. 21 is twenty-one; 20 is twenty."}],"explain":{"kid":["A numeral is the written mark for a number. 7 means seven things.","Teen numbers have one ten and some ones. 14 is a ten and four ones.","When you write sixteen, put 1 then 6. The left digit is the tens. The right digit is the ones.","Watch out: 12 and 21 use the same digits, but they are different numbers because the places swapped."],"adult":["CCSS K.CC.3: write numbers from 0 to 20 and represent a number of objects with a written numeral. NC Year 1: read and write numbers from 1 to 20 in numerals. Words: numeral, digit, tens, ones, teen, place value, number line.","Common mistakes: swapping teen digits (12 read as twenty-one); writing 15 as 51; thinking 20 means two ones; treating teen numbers as 'ten and teen' words without linking to 10+n; counting objects but tapping a nearby wrong numeral.","In the lab your child matches dots to numerals, builds a teen with a ten-rod and ones, writes digits on a pad, places a bead on a 0–20 line, picks 12 not 21 for twelve, and reads 16 as sixteen."]},"familyTip":"Write 12 and 21 on two cards. Ask 'which is twelve?' then build each with a bundle of ten straws plus loose ones.","keyIdea":"Reading a numeral means knowing which written mark stands for a count from 0 to 20. Writing means making those digits in the right order: tens on the left, ones on the right. Teen numbers are one ten and some ones — 14 is 1 ten and 4 ones. Swapping digits changes the number: 12 is twelve, 21 is twenty-one.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Teen (11–19) or not? Drag each numeral, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Teen (11-19)","Not a teen"],"items":[["14",0],["10",1],["17",0],["20",1],["11",0],["9",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagto20","caption":"Flagship lab: match counts to numerals, build teens as ten-and-ones, write digits, place on a 0–20 line, and catch 12/21 swaps.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_8gy7uxRlF6":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Swap 10 tens for 1 hundred. Ones now?","choices":["The same","10 fewer","10 more"],"answer":0,"hint":"Regrouping does not add or remove ones.","explain":"Same 100 ones — just shown as one flat."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"130 is…","choices":["1 hundred and 3 tens","13 ones","3 hundreds and 1 ten"],"answer":0,"hint":"Split into hundreds and tens.","explain":"130 means 1 hundred and 3 tens: 1×100 + 3×10."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"1 hundred vs 12 tens — more is…","choices":["12 tens","1 hundred","equal"],"answer":0,"hint":"12×10 = 120; 1×100 = 100.","explain":"12 tens are 120 ones, which beats 1 hundred (100 ones)."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A hundred is…","choices":["ten tens","ten ones","one ten"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of ten rods joined into one flat.","explain":"A hundred equals ten tens — that is the hundreds place."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"{t} tens = how many ones?","answer":"t*10","hint":"Each ten-rod holds exactly 10 ones.","explain":"{t} tens make {=t*10} ones in all.","params":{"t":[3,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"{h} hundreds = how many tens?","answer":"h*10","hint":"Each hundred holds 10 tens.","explain":"{h} hundreds = {=h*10} tens.","params":{"h":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why regroup tens into a hundred?","choices":["same total, clearer place value","to make the number smaller","because tens disappear"],"answer":0,"hint":"Place value shows groups without changing amount.","explain":"Regrouping clarifies hundreds/tens; the total stays."},{"type":"order","id":"m8","prompt":"Order: read a hundreds-and-tens number.","items":["count the hundred flats","count the ten rods","say hundreds then tens as one number"],"hint":"Always read the hundreds digit first.","explain":"Read hundreds, then tens, as one number (e.g. 130)."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"10 tens make…","choices":["1 hundred","10 ones","1 ten"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ten rods of ten join into one hundred.","explain":"Ten tens = one hundred. That is the hundreds place."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"1 hundred and 1 ten is…","choices":["110","11","101"],"answer":0,"hint":"Hundreds first, then tens.","explain":"1×100 + 1×10 = 110, not 11 or 101."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"How many tens make {h} hundred?","answer":"h*10","hint":"Each hundred is made of exactly 10 tens.","explain":"{h} hundred = {=h*10} tens.","params":{"h":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"{h} hundred and {t} tens = ?","answer":"h*100+t*10","hint":"Hundreds ×100, tens ×10, then add.","explain":"{h}×100 + {t}×10 = {=h*100+t*10}.","params":{"h":[1,2],"t":[0,4]}},{"type":"order","id":"p5","prompt":"Order the steps to regroup tens into a hundred.","items":["gather 10 tens","swap them for 1 hundred flat","check the total is still the same"],"hint":"Collect tens first, then swap, then check.","explain":"Ten tens regroup into one hundred without changing how many ones you have."}],"explain":{"kid":["A hundred is ten tens stuck together.","One tall ten-rod is 10. Ten of those rods make 100 — a hundred flat.","When you swap 10 tens for 1 hundred, you still have the same number of ones.","Read hundreds first, then tens: 1 hundred and 3 tens is 130."],"adult":["NC Y2 / CCSS 2.NBT.A.1: 100 is a bundle of ten tens; the hundreds place counts units of 100. Words: hundred, ten, flat, rod, regroup, place value.","Common mistakes: treating a hundred as ten ones; reading 1 hundred and 1 ten as 11; thinking regrouping changes the total; saying more pieces always means more value.","In the lab your child bundles 10 tens into a flat, reads 110, swaps tens for a hundred (total stays 100), builds 100, and sees 12 tens beat 1 hundred."]},"familyTip":"Build 100 with ten ten-rods, then swap for one flat. Ask: 'Still 100 ones?' Try reading 130 as 1 hundred and 3 tens.","keyIdea":"One hundred is ten tens. In place value, the hundreds digit counts groups of 100 — ten rods of ten make one hundred flat.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Hundred = ten tens — fair idea or myth? Drag each clue, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Fair idea","Myth / mix-up"],"items":[["10 tens make 1 hundred",0],["1 hundred and 3 tens = 130",0],["swapping 10 tens for 1 hundred keeps the total",0],["a hundred is ten ones",1],["1 hundred and 1 ten = 11",1],["12 tens = 120 ones",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtenten","caption":"Flagship lab: bundle 10 tens into a hundred, read 110, swap without changing total, build 100, compare flats vs tens, then challenge.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_R2ccrI-nKD":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"700 has how many tens?","choices":["0 tens","7 tens","70 tens"],"answer":0,"hint":"Tens place is empty in a multiple of 100.","explain":"700 = 7 hundreds, 0 tens, 0 ones."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"5 hundreds is written…","choices":["500","50","5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Hundreds need two zeros after the digit.","explain":"5×100 = 500, not 50 or 5."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A multiple of 100…","choices":["ends with two zeros","ends with one zero","can end with any digit"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of 100, 200, 300 — each ends the same way.","explain":"Empty tens and ones show as two zeros."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"900 means…","choices":["9 hundreds, 0 tens, 0 ones","9 tens","90 ones"],"answer":0,"hint":"Read the hundreds digit, then the zeros.","explain":"900 is nine hundreds only."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"{h}00 has how many hundreds?","answer":"h","hint":"Look at the hundreds digit.","explain":"{h}00 contains {h} hundreds.","params":{"h":[3,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"What number is {h} hundreds?","answer":"h*100","hint":"Multiply the hundreds digit by 100.","explain":"{h} hundreds make the number {=h*100} with two zeros.","params":{"h":[1,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why write two zeros in 600?","choices":["show empty tens and ones","to make it look longer","because tens disappeared forever"],"answer":0,"hint":"Zeros mark empty places in place value.","explain":"Zeros show 0 tens and 0 ones; the value is 6 hundreds."},{"type":"order","id":"m8","prompt":"Order: place 400 on a 0–1000 line.","items":["find the hundreds marks","count four marks from 0","put 400 on that mark"],"hint":"Use the big hundred marks, not the tens.","explain":"400 sits on the fourth hundred mark from 0."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"300 means…","choices":["3 hundreds, 0 tens, 0 ones","3 tens","30 ones"],"answer":0,"hint":"Whole hundreds only — empty tens and ones.","explain":"300 = 3×100 with 0 tens and 0 ones."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which is greater?","choices":["400","40","They are equal"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare place value, not digit look-alikes.","explain":"400 is 4 hundreds; 40 is only 4 tens."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"{h} hundreds = ?","answer":"h*100","hint":"Each hundred is worth 100. Multiply.","explain":"{h} hundreds make {=h*100}.","params":{"h":[2,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"How many hundreds in {n}00?","answer":"n","hint":"The first digit counts the hundreds.","explain":"{n}00 means {n} hundreds.","params":{"n":[1,9]}},{"type":"order","id":"p5","prompt":"Order: show 700 with place value.","items":["put 7 in the hundreds place","put 0 in tens","put 0 in ones"],"hint":"Fill hundreds first, then the empty places.","explain":"700 shows 7 hundreds with empty tens and ones."}],"explain":{"kid":["A multiple of 100 is made of whole hundreds only.","300 means 3 hundreds, 0 tens, and 0 ones — it ends with two zeros.","400 is much more than 40 because the 4 sits in the hundreds place, not the tens place.","On a line to 1000, multiples of 100 sit on the big marks: 100, 200, 300…"],"adult":["CCSS 2.NBT.1.b / NC Y2: 100–900 as amounts of hundreds with empty tens and ones. Words: hundred, multiple, place value, digit.","Common mistakes: reading 400 as 4 tens; writing 5 hundreds as 50; thinking extra zeros are only for looks; placing 700 near 70 on a 0–1000 line.","In the lab your child builds flats, fills a hundreds-tens-ones chart, marks the line, compares 400 vs 40, and chooses 500 for five hundreds."]},"familyTip":"Build 300 with three hundred flats. Ask: 'Any tens or ones left?' Compare 400 and 40 on paper.","keyIdea":"Multiples of 100 (100–900) are whole hundreds: a hundreds digit with 0 tens and 0 ones. 300 means 3 hundreds, not 3 tens.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Multiple of 100 — fair idea or mix-up? Drag each clue, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Fair idea","Myth / mix-up"],"items":[["300 means 3 hundreds, 0 tens, 0 ones",0],["700 ends with two zeros",0],["400 is greater than 40",0],["400 means 4 tens",1],["5 hundreds is written 50",1],["900 sits on the 900 mark to 1000",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagm100","caption":"Flagship lab: build flats, chart hundreds, mark 700, compare 400 vs 40, then challenge.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_aPBzD28_mT":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"305 is…","choices":["3 hundreds + 0 tens + 5 ones","3 + 0 + 5 = 8","just 35"],"answer":0,"hint":"Zero can hold the tens place.","explain":"305 = 3 hundreds + 0 tens + 5 ones."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"516 needs…","choices":["5 hundreds + 1 ten + 6 ones","1 hundred + 5 tens + 6 ones","5 + 1 + 6 ones only"],"answer":0,"hint":"Match each digit to its place.","explain":"516 = 5 hundreds + 1 ten + 6 ones."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"400 is…","choices":["4 hundreds + 0 tens + 0 ones","4 tens","40 with decoration zeros"],"answer":0,"hint":"A multiple of 100 still has three places.","explain":"400 = 4 hundreds + 0 tens + 0 ones."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which is the mix-up?","choices":["347 means 3 + 4 + 7 = 14","Left digit counts hundreds","Middle digit counts tens"],"answer":0,"hint":"Spot the digit-summing mix-up among the choices.","explain":"Digits name place values; summing them as ones is the mix-up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"812 expands to…","choices":["8 hundreds + 1 ten + 2 ones","8 + 1 + 2 = 11","2 hundreds + 1 ten + 8 ones"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do not reverse or sum the digits.","explain":"812 = 8 hundreds + 1 ten + 2 ones."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Compose: {h} hundreds, {t} tens, {o} ones make…","answer":"h*100+t*10+o","hint":"Place values multiply then add.","explain":"{h}×100 + {t}×10 + {o} = {=h*100+t*10+o}.","params":{"h":[2,7],"t":[1,6],"o":[0,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"The tens digit of 264 is…","choices":["6","2","4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Tens sit in the middle place.","explain":"In 264 the tens digit is 6."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Place value for three digits means…","choices":["each digit’s worth depends on hundreds, tens or ones place","add the three digits","always swap left and right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the hundreds–tens–ones chart places.","explain":"The same digit means hundreds, tens or ones depending on place."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"How many ones in {n} (ones digit)?","answer":"n%10","hint":"The ones digit is the rightmost digit of the number.","explain":"{n} has ones digit {=n%10}.","params":{"n":[123,678]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"2 hundreds + 4 tens + 1 one equals…","choices":["241","7","214"],"answer":0,"hint":"Hundreds are worth 100 each. Do not add the digits as ones.","explain":"2×100 + 4×10 + 1 = 241. Summing to 7 or swapping places is a mix-up."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"347 expands to…","choices":["3 hundreds + 4 tens + 7 ones","3 + 4 + 7 = 14","7 hundreds + 4 tens + 3 ones"],"answer":0,"hint":"Left = hundreds, middle = tens, right = ones.","explain":"347 = 3 hundreds + 4 tens + 7 ones."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"The 5 in 520 means…","choices":["5 hundreds (500)","5 tens","5 + 2 + 0"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which place is the 5 sitting in?","explain":"The 5 is in the hundreds place, so it means five hundreds."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"{h} hundreds + {t} tens + {o} ones = ?","answer":"h*100+t*10+o","hint":"100×hundreds + 10×tens + ones.","explain":"{h}×100 + {t}×10 + {o} = {=h*100+t*10+o}.","params":{"h":[1,5],"t":[0,8],"o":[0,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"How many hundreds in {n}?","answer":"(n-n%100)/100","hint":"The left digit of a three-digit number.","explain":"{n} has {=(n-n%100)/100} hundreds.","params":{"n":[105,890]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A three-digit number has hundreds, tens and ones places.","The left digit tells how many hundreds. The middle tells tens. The right tells ones.","347 means 3 hundreds, 4 tens and 7 ones — not 3 plus 4 plus 7.","A zero can hold a place, like the 0 tens in 305."],"adult":["CCSS 2.NBT.1: understand that the three digits of a three-digit number represent amounts of hundreds, tens and ones; 100 can be thought of as a bundle of ten tens (called a hundred). Distinct from The two digits of a two-digit number, A Hundred Is Ten Tens (bundling), Counting within 1,000 (hops), and The multiples of 100.","Words: hundreds, tens, ones, place value, expand, zero that holds a place. Common mistakes: summing digits; swapping places; reading the hundreds digit as tens or ones; ignoring zero as a place holder.","In the lab your child builds 241 on an H–T–O chart, expands 347, names what the hundreds digit is worth, reads 305 with a zero tens place, spots the digit-sum mix-up, and builds 516."]},"familyTip":"Write 347. Ask: how many hundred flats? How many ten rods? How many ones? Build it with base-ten blocks if you have them.","keyIdea":"In a three-digit number the digits stand for hundreds, tens and ones — so 347 means 3 hundreds + 4 tens + 7 ones, not 3+4+7 and not a swap of places.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Three-digit place idea or mix-up? Drag each clue, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["True place-value idea","Myth / mix-up"],"items":[["left digit counts hundreds",0],["middle digit counts tens",0],["347 means 3 + 4 + 7 = 14",1],["305 is 3 hundreds + 0 tens + 5 ones",0],["347 means 7 hundreds + 4 tens + 3 ones",1],["a zero can hold an empty place",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flag3digit","caption":"Flagship lab: build 241 on H–T–O, expand 347, name hundreds-digit worth, read 305 with zero, catch digit-sum myth, build 516.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_THl9GLxwoL":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"14 is…","choices":["1 ten + 4 ones","1 + 4 = 5","4 tens + 1 one"],"answer":0,"hint":"Teens still use tens and ones places.","explain":"14 = 1 ten + 4 ones. Teens still use the tens place."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"52 needs…","choices":["5 tens + 2 ones","2 tens + 5 ones","5 + 2 ones only"],"answer":0,"hint":"Left digit tens; right digit ones.","explain":"52 = 5 tens + 2 ones on the place chart."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"60 is…","choices":["6 tens + 0 ones","6 ones","0 tens + 6 ones"],"answer":0,"hint":"A decade is that many tens and zero ones.","explain":"60 = 6 tens + 0 ones — a decade with empty ones."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which is the mix-up?","choices":["47 means 4 + 7 = 11","Left digit counts tens","Right digit counts ones"],"answer":0,"hint":"Spot the digit-summing mix-up in the choices.","explain":"Digits name place values; summing them as ones is the mix-up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"81 expands to…","choices":["8 tens + 1 one","8 + 1 = 9","1 ten + 8 ones"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do not reverse or sum the digits.","explain":"81 = 8 tens + 1 one; do not reverse the digits."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Compose: {t} tens and {o} ones make…","answer":"t*10+o","hint":"Multiply tens by 10, then add the ones digit.","explain":"{t}×10 + {o} = {=t*10+o}.","params":{"t":[2,8],"o":[1,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"The ones digit of 56 is…","choices":["6","5","11"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ones sit on the right of a two-digit number.","explain":"In 56 the ones digit is 6; the tens digit is 5."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Place value means…","choices":["a digit’s worth depends on its place","add the two digits","swap left and right always"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think tens place vs ones place.","explain":"The same digit means tens or ones depending on place."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"How many ones in {n} (the ones digit)?","answer":"n%10","hint":"Rightmost digit of a two-digit number.","explain":"{n} has ones digit {=n%10}.","params":{"n":[23,78]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"3 tens + 5 ones equals…","choices":["35","8","53"],"answer":0,"hint":"Tens are worth ten each. Do not add the digits as ones.","explain":"3×10 + 5 = 35. Adding digits to 8 or reversing to 53 are mix-ups."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"47 expands to…","choices":["4 tens + 7 ones","4 + 7 = 11","7 tens + 4 ones"],"answer":0,"hint":"Left digit = tens; right digit = ones.","explain":"47 = 4 tens + 7 ones. Do not sum or reverse the digits."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"The 2 in 29 means…","choices":["2 tens (twenty)","2 ones","2 + 9"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which place is the 2 sitting in?","explain":"The 2 is in the tens place, so it means two tens — twenty."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"{t} tens + {o} ones = ?","answer":"t*10+o","hint":"Each ten is worth 10. Multiply tens by 10, then add ones.","explain":"{t}×10 + {o} = {=t*10+o}.","params":{"t":[1,6],"o":[0,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"How many tens in {n}?","answer":"(n-n%10)/10","hint":"The left digit of a two-digit number.","explain":"{n} has {=(n-n%10)/10} tens (and {=n%10} ones).","params":{"n":[12,89]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A two-digit number has a tens place and an ones place.","The left digit tells how many tens. The right digit tells how many ones.","47 means 4 tens and 7 ones — forty-seven — not 4 plus 7.","A teen like 14 still follows the rule: 1 ten and 4 ones."],"adult":["CCSS 1.NBT.2: understand that the two digits of a two-digit number represent amounts of tens and ones; 10 can be thought of as a bundle of ten ones; teens are 1 ten + some ones; decades are that many tens and 0 ones. Distinct from A Ten Is Ten Ones (bundling action) and A Hundred Is Ten Tens.","Words: tens, ones, place value, expand, digit. Common mistakes: adding the digits (47 as 11); reversing places (47 as 74’s places); reading the tens digit as that many ones.","In the lab your child builds 35 on a place chart, expands 47, names what the tens digit is worth, expands 14, spots the digit-sum mix-up, and builds 52."]},"familyTip":"Point at 47 on a door or bus. Ask: how many tens rods? How many ones? Build it with sticks of ten and loose cubes.","keyIdea":"In a two-digit number the left digit counts tens and the right digit counts ones — so 47 means 4 tens + 7 ones (forty-seven), not 4+7 and not 7 tens.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Place-value idea or mix-up? Drag each clue, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["True place-value idea","Myth / mix-up"],"items":[["left digit counts tens",0],["right digit counts ones",0],["47 means 4 + 7 = 11",1],["14 is 1 ten + 4 ones",0],["47 means 7 tens + 4 ones",1],["the tens digit is worth that many tens",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flag2digit","caption":"Flagship lab: build 35 on a tens/ones chart, expand 47, name the tens digit’s worth, expand a teen, catch digit-sum myth, build 52.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_kw7xmp68rU":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is 10 less than 81?","choices":["71","80","91"],"answer":0,"hint":"Take one ten from 8 tens. The 1 one stays.","explain":"81 is 8 tens and 1 one. One ten less is 7 tens and 1 one: 71. 80 is only 1 less, and 91 is 10 more."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Sam has {=a*10+b} stickers. He gets a pack of 10 more. How many now?","answer":"a*10+b+10","hint":"A pack of 10 is one more ten. Change the tens digit.","explain":"{=a*10+b} plus one ten is {=a+1} tens and {b} ones, so Sam has {=a*10+b+10} stickers.","params":{"a":[1,8],"b":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A shop has {=a*10+b} apples. It sells a bag of 10. How many are left?","answer":"a*10+b-10","hint":"Selling a bag of 10 is taking one ten away.","explain":"{=a*10+b} take away one ten is {=a-1} tens and {b} ones. That leaves {=a*10+b-10} apples.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Mia says 10 more than 48 is 49. What did she do?","choices":["She added 1, not 10","She is right","She took 10 away"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at which digit Mia changed.","explain":"49 is 1 more: Mia changed the ones digit. 10 more changes the tens digit, so the answer is 58."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which number is just above 67 on a hundred square?","choices":["57","77","66"],"answer":0,"hint":"Going up one row takes 10 away.","explain":"One row up is 10 less, so 57 is above 67. 77 is below it, and 66 is to its left."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which pair shows 10 more?","choices":["29 and 39","29 and 30","29 and 28"],"answer":0,"hint":"In a jump of 10 the ones digit stays the same.","explain":"29 to 39 keeps 9 ones and adds a ten. 29 to 30 is 1 more, and 28 is 1 less."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What is the quickest way to find 10 more than 45?","choices":["Make the 4 into a 5: 55","Count on 10 fingers","Add 1 to get 46"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think tens and ones. You do not need to count in ones.","explain":"45 is 4 tens and 5 ones. One more ten makes 5 tens: 55. Counting fingers works but is slow, and 46 is only 1 more."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"What is 10 more than 90?","choices":["100","91","80"],"answer":0,"hint":"9 tens and one more ten make 10 tens.","explain":"10 tens is one hundred, so 10 more than 90 is 100. 91 is 1 more and 80 is 10 less."},{"type":"order","id":"m9","prompt":"Put these in order, each one 10 more than the last.","items":["14","24","34","44"],"hint":"Keep the 4 ones. Make the tens digit go up by 1 each time.","explain":"14, 24, 34, 44: the ones digit stays 4 and the tens digit counts 1, 2, 3, 4."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What is 10 more than 56?","choices":["66","57","46"],"answer":0,"hint":"Add one ten. Which digit counts the tens?","explain":"56 has 5 tens. One more ten makes 6 tens, and the 6 ones stay. So it is 66. 57 is only 1 more."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"What is 10 more than {=a*10+b}?","answer":"a*10+b+10","hint":"Make the tens digit one bigger. Keep the ones digit.","explain":"{=a*10+b} has {a} tens and {b} ones. One more ten gives {=a+1} tens and {b} ones, which is {=a*10+b+10}.","params":{"a":[1,8],"b":[0,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"What is 10 less than {=a*10+b}?","answer":"a*10+b-10","hint":"Make the tens digit one smaller. Keep the ones digit.","explain":"{=a*10+b} has {a} tens. Take one ten away to leave {=a-1} tens and {b} ones, which is {=a*10+b-10}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[0,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"You find 10 less than 73. Which digit changes?","choices":["The tens digit","The ones digit","Both digits"],"answer":0,"hint":"10 less takes away one ten, not one single.","explain":"73 is 7 tens and 3 ones. Taking a ten leaves 6 tens and 3 ones: 63. Only the tens digit changed."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"On a hundred square, where is 10 more than 34?","choices":["The box just below 34","The box just right of 34","The box just above 34"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each row has 10 numbers. Which way do the numbers get bigger by 10?","explain":"Going down one row adds 10, so 44 sits just below 34. The box to the right is 35, only 1 more."}],"explain":{"kid":["A ten is a group of 10. To find 10 more, add one ten. To find 10 less, take one ten away.","Look at 56. It has 5 tens and 6 ones. Add a ten and you get 6 tens and 6 ones. That is 66.","Only the tens digit moves, by one. The ones digit stays the same. So 10 less than 56 is 46.","On a hundred square, 10 more is the box just under the number. 10 less is the box just above it. You do not need to count."],"adult":["Ages 6 to 7. CCSS 1.NBT.5: given a two-digit number, mentally find 10 more or 10 less without having to count, and explain the reasoning. In England, Year 2 place value (tens and ones) leads to the Year 3 aim to find 10 or 100 more or less than a given number.","The reason is place value. A two-digit number is tens and ones, so adding or removing one ten changes only the tens digit. On a hundred square each row holds 10 numbers, which puts 10 more directly below.","Common mistakes: counting on 10 single steps on fingers, which is slow and error-prone; giving 57 for 10 more than 56 because the ones digit was changed; going the wrong way on the square; and changing both digits.","In the lab your child jumps down rows, adds and removes ten rods, sorts 10 more, 10 less and 1 more cards, then rebuilds a cut-out piece of the hundred square."]},"familyTip":"Point at a house number or a page number, like 34. Ask: what is 10 more? What is 10 less? Then check on a hundred square or with bundles of 10 straws.","keyIdea":"To find 10 more or 10 less than a two-digit number, change the tens digit by 1 and keep the ones digit the same: 10 more than 56 is 66, and 10 less is 46. On a hundred square, 10 more is the number straight below and 10 less is straight above, so there is no need to count on in ones.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Start at 52. Is each number 10 more, 10 less, or not a jump of 10? Drag each card, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["10 more than 52","10 less than 52","Not a jump of 10"],"items":[["62",0],["42",1],["53",2],["51",2],["63",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtenjump","caption":"Flagship lab: jump a row on the hundred square, add and take tens, sort 10 more, 10 less and 1 more, then fix a piece of the square.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_jY7uf0Cb7o":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the 6 in 6,218 worth?","choices":["6000","600","6"],"answer":0,"hint":"The 6 is the first of four digits. Which place is that?","explain":"The 6 is in the thousands place, so it is worth 6000. 600 would put it one place too far right."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A shop has {a} boxes of 1000 beads, {b} bags of 100 beads and {c} loose beads. How many beads?","answer":"a*1000+b*100+c","hint":"There are no tens. Which digit holds the tens place?","explain":"{a} thousands, {b} hundreds, 0 tens and {c} ones. With a 0 in the tens place that is {=a*1000+b*100+c} beads.","params":{"a":[1,9],"b":[1,9],"c":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"What is the tens digit of {=a*1000+b*100+c*10+d} worth?","answer":"c*10","hint":"The tens digit is second from the right.","explain":"The tens digit is {c}, so it is worth {c} × 10 = {=c*10}.","params":{"a":[1,9],"b":[0,9],"c":[1,9],"d":[0,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Ben writes 5 thousands and 7 ones as 57. What should it be?","choices":["5,007","5,070","507"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write a digit for every place. Empty places get a 0.","explain":"5 thousands, 0 hundreds, 0 tens, 7 ones is 5,007. Ben left out the two zeros, so his 5 slid into the tens place."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which digit in 2,946 is in the hundreds place?","choices":["9","2","4"],"answer":0,"hint":"From the right: ones, tens, hundreds.","explain":"6 is ones, 4 is tens and 9 is hundreds. The 2 is thousands. So the 9 is worth 900."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which is the expanded form of 8,305?","choices":["8000 + 300 + 5","8000 + 300 + 50","800 + 30 + 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write each digit's value. The 0 tens add nothing.","explain":"8 thousands = 8000, 3 hundreds = 300, 0 tens = 0 and 5 ones = 5. So 8,305 = 8000 + 300 + 5."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"How many hundreds make one thousand?","choices":["10","100","1000"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each place is worth 10 times the place on its right.","explain":"Ten 100s make 1000, just as ten 10s make 100 and ten 1s make 10."},{"type":"order","id":"m8","prompt":"Put these numbers in order, smallest first.","items":["3,099","3,190","3,901","9,013"],"hint":"Compare thousands first. If they match, compare hundreds, then tens.","explain":"9,013 has the most thousands, so it is largest. The others have 3 thousands; 0, 1 and 9 hundreds put them in order: 3,099, 3,190, 3,901."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"In 4,444, the thousands 4 is how many times the hundreds 4?","choices":["10 times","4 times","1000 times"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write the value of each 4, then compare 4000 with 400.","explain":"The thousands 4 is worth 4000 and the hundreds 4 is worth 400. 4000 is 10 times 400, one place to the left."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"In 7,345, what is the 7 worth?","choices":["7000","7","700"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which place is the 7 in? Count the places from the right: ones, tens, hundreds, thousands.","explain":"The 7 is in the thousands place, so it is worth 7 thousands: 7000. Just '7' forgets its place."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"What number is {a}000 + {b}00 + {c}0 + {d}?","answer":"a*1000+b*100+c*10+d","hint":"Put each part in its own place: thousands, hundreds, tens, ones.","explain":"{a} thousands, {b} hundreds, {c} tens and {d} ones make {=a*1000+b*100+c*10+d}.","params":{"a":[1,9],"b":[1,9],"c":[1,9],"d":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"In the number {=a*1000+b*100+c*10+d}, what is the hundreds digit worth?","answer":"b*100","hint":"Find the second digit from the left. It counts hundreds.","explain":"The hundreds digit is {b}. It counts hundreds, so it is worth {b} × 100 = {=b*100}.","params":{"a":[1,9],"b":[1,9],"c":[0,9],"d":[0,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which number has 4 thousands, 0 hundreds, 6 tens and 2 ones?","choices":["4,062","462","4,602"],"answer":0,"hint":"There are no hundreds. What digit holds an empty place?","explain":"Write 4 for thousands, 0 for hundreds, 6 for tens and 2 for ones: 4,062. Without the 0 you get 462, which is only 4 hundreds."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"13 hundreds is the same as …","choices":["1 thousand and 3 hundreds","13 thousands","1 hundred and 3 tens"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ten 100s make one 1000. How many hundreds are left over?","explain":"Ten of the 13 hundreds make 1 thousand, and 3 hundreds are left. So 13 hundreds is 1,300."}],"explain":{"kid":["A four-digit number has four places. From the left they are thousands, hundreds, tens and ones.","The place tells you what a digit is worth. In 7,345 the 7 is in the thousands place. So it is worth 7000, not 7.","Each place is worth 10 times the place on its right. Ten 100s make one 1000.","If a place is empty, we write 0 to hold it. 3,052 has no hundreds. Without the 0 it would be 352."],"adult":["Ages 8 to 9. National Curriculum Year 4: recognise the place value of each digit in a four-digit number (thousands, hundreds, tens and ones). CCSS 4.NBT.1 and 4.NBT.2: a digit in one place represents ten times what it represents in the place to its right; read and write numbers in expanded form.","A digit's value is the digit times its place: in 6,218 the 6 is worth 6 × 1000 = 6000. Expanded form writes the number as the sum of these values: 6000 + 200 + 10 + 8.","Common mistakes: giving the digit instead of its value (the 7 in 7,345 is 'worth 7'); leaving out a zero that holds an empty place (writing 5 thousands and 7 ones as 57); writing jumbled parts in the order given (80 + 6000 + 9 + 700 as 8697); and thinking 13 hundreds can sit in one place instead of exchanging ten 100s for a 1000.","In the lab your child builds numbers with place value counters, taps each 5 in 5,555, builds 3,052, exchanges ten 100 counters for one 1000, and drops jumbled parts into their columns."]},"familyTip":"Read a four-digit number together, like a year (2026) or a price. Point at one digit and ask: what is this digit worth? Then say it as parts: 2000 + 0 + 20 + 6.","keyIdea":"In a four-digit number each digit sits in a place: thousands, hundreds, tens or ones. The place sets the digit's value, so in 7,345 the 7 is worth 7000, the 3 is worth 300, the 4 is worth 40 and the 5 is worth 5. Each place is worth 10 times the place to its right, and a 0 holds an empty place (3,052 is not 352).","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Where is the 7 in each number? Drag each card to the place of its 7, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["thousands","hundreds","tens","ones"],"items":[["7,312",0],["2,701",1],["1,374",2],["4,217",3],["7,005",0],["9,876",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagpvc","caption":"Flagship lab: build with place value counters, value each 5 in 5,555, hold a place with 0, swap ten 100s for 1000, sort parts.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_U0waNfD8PB":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which is less: 81 or 18?","choices":["18","81","They match"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare tens: 8 tens versus 1 ten.","explain":"18 has 1 ten. 81 has 8 tens. So 18 < 81."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which symbol fits: 55 ? 55","choices":["=",">","<"],"answer":0,"hint":"Same tens and same ones means equal.","explain":"Both numbers are 5 tens and 5 ones, so 55 = 55."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"What is 10 more than {=a*10+b}?","answer":"a*10+b+10","hint":"10 more means the tens digit goes up by 1. Ones stay the same.","explain":"{=a*10+b} plus one ten is {=a*10+b+10}.","params":{"a":[2,8],"b":[0,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Why is 34 less than 43?","choices":["34 has fewer tens","The digits add to the same sum","3 is a taller digit than 4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Place value: tens decide before ones or digit sums.","explain":"34 has 3 tens; 43 has 4 tens. Digit sums matching does not make the numbers equal."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"70 ? 69 — which symbol?","choices":[">","<","="],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the tens digits: 7 tens versus 6 tens. Ones do not override tens.","explain":"70 has 7 tens; 69 has 6 tens. So 70 > 69 even though 69 has more ones."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"What is the ones digit of {=10*a+b}?","answer":"b","hint":"The ones digit is the right digit.","explain":"{=10*a+b} has ones digit {b}.","params":{"a":[1,9],"b":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Order 29, 58, 71. Which comes first (smallest)?","choices":["29","58","71"],"answer":0,"hint":"The smallest has the fewest tens.","explain":"2 tens is fewer than 5 or 7, so 29 is smallest."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A mix-up says 34 > 43 because 3+4 = 4+3. What went wrong?","choices":["Digit sums do not decide size","The symbols were swapped","Ones should be compared first"],"answer":0,"hint":"Place value, not digit sum, decides which number is greater.","explain":"Adding digits is not how we compare. Tens first: 3 < 4, so 34 < 43."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which is greater: 34 or 43?","choices":["43","34","They are equal"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the tens digit of each number first.","explain":"43 has 4 tens. 34 has only 3 tens. More tens wins, so 43 > 34."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which symbol fits: 47 ? 74","choices":["<",">","="],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the tens: 4 tens versus 7 tens.","explain":"4 tens is less than 7 tens, so 47 < 74. The open side of < faces the greater number."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"What number is {a} tens and {b} ones?","answer":"a*10+b","hint":"Tens are worth 10 each. Multiply the tens digit by 10, then add the ones.","explain":"{a} tens and {b} ones make {=a*10+b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[0,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"58 and 52 have the same tens. Which is greater?","choices":["58","52","They match"],"answer":0,"hint":"Same tens means compare the ones digits next.","explain":"Both have 5 tens. 8 ones beat 2 ones, so 58 > 52."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"How many tens does {=10*a+b} have?","answer":"a","hint":"The tens digit is the left digit of a two-digit number.","explain":"{=10*a+b} is {a} tens and {b} ones. The tens digit is {a}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[0,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A two-digit number has tens and ones. The left digit counts tens. The right digit counts ones.","To compare two numbers, look at the tens first. More tens means a greater number.","If the tens are the same, compare the ones. 58 is greater than 52 because 8 ones beat 2 ones.","Write >, = or <. The open side of > faces the greater number. Equal means the same tens and the same ones."],"adult":["Ages 6 to 7. CCSS 1.NBT.3: compare two two-digit numbers based on meanings of the tens and ones digits, recording the results of comparisons with the symbols >, =, and <. National Curriculum Year 2 number and place value: compare and order numbers from 0 up to 100; use <, > and = signs.","The tens-first rule is place value, not digit size as a glyph and not the sum of the digits. 34 < 43 even though both digit-sums are 7, because 3 tens is less than 4 tens. When tens match, the ones digit decides: 58 > 52.","Common mistakes: adding the digits and calling the sums equal (3+4 = 4+3 so 34 and 43 'match'); reading a number backwards (34 as 43); choosing the number with the taller-looking digit; putting the open side of > toward the smaller number; and treating 40 as equal to 4.","In the lab your child compares with tens rods and ones cubes, picks > = < for a pair, drags three numbers into smallest-to-largest order, and catches the digit-sum myth."]},"familyTip":"Show two two-digit numbers (a door number and a bus number work well). Ask: which has more tens? If the tens match, which has more ones? Then write the comparison with > or <.","keyIdea":"Compare two two-digit numbers by place value: look at the tens digit first, then the ones if the tens match. Write the result with >, = or <, and order a set from smallest to largest the same way.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which symbol fits? Drag each pair into >, = or <, or tap the pair then a box.","opts":{"bins":[">","=","<"],"items":[["47 ? 74",2],["81 ? 18",0],["55 ? 55",1],["36 ? 63",2],["70 ? 69",0],["22 ? 22",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcmp2d","caption":"Flagship lab: tens-first compare, ones when tens match, choose > = <, order three numbers, catch digit-sum myths.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_NLSfvB9vUl":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Round 65 to the nearest 10.","choices":["60","70","65"],"answer":1,"hint":"65 is exactly halfway between 60 and 70.","explain":"A number exactly halfway rounds up, so 65 rounds to 70."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which digit do you look at to round 5,283 to the nearest 100?","choices":["the tens digit, 8","the ones digit, 3","the thousands digit, 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look one place to the right of the hundreds.","explain":"To round to the nearest 100, look at the tens digit. It is 8, so 5,283 rounds up to 5,300."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Round {a} to the nearest 1000.","answer":"a-a%1000+1000*(a%1000>=500)","hint":"Look at the hundreds digit. 5 or more rounds up.","explain":"{a} rounds to {=a-a%1000+1000*(a%1000>=500)}. The hundreds digit decides.","params":{"a":[1100,9800]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Round 4,367 to the nearest 10.","choices":["4,360","4,370","4,400"],"answer":1,"hint":"Rounding to the nearest 10 means looking at the ones digit.","explain":"The ones digit is 7, so round the tens up: 4,370. 4,400 is the nearest 100."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Max says 449 rounded to the nearest 100 is 500, because 449 rounds to 450, and 450 rounds to 500. What went wrong?","choices":["he rounded twice; 449 is nearer 400","nothing, he is right","he should round to 450"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is 449 above or below halfway, 450?","explain":"449 is 49 from 400 and 51 from 500, so it rounds to 400. Rounding in two steps moved it the wrong way."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Use rounding to the nearest 100 to estimate 612 + 289.","choices":["about 900","about 800","about 1,000"],"answer":0,"hint":"Round each number first, then add.","explain":"612 rounds to 600 and 289 rounds to 300. 600 + 300 = 900. The exact answer is 901."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A number rounds to {a}0 to the nearest 10. What is the smallest whole number it could be?","answer":"a*10-5","hint":"Which numbers below {a}0 still round up to it?","explain":"{=a*10-5} is the smallest, because it is exactly halfway and halfway rounds up. {=a*10-6} would round down.","params":{"a":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which number rounds to 2,000 to the nearest 1000?","choices":["2,499","2,500","1,499"],"answer":0,"hint":"Halfway between 2,000 and 3,000 is 2,500.","explain":"2,499 is below halfway, so it rounds down to 2,000. 2,500 rounds up to 3,000 and 1,499 rounds to 1,000."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Round 74 to the nearest 10.","choices":["70","80","74"],"answer":0,"hint":"74 is between 70 and 80. Which is nearer?","explain":"74 is 4 from 70 and 6 from 80, so it rounds to 70. The ones digit 4 is less than 5."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Round {a} to the nearest 10.","answer":"a-a%10+10*(a%10>=5)","hint":"Look at the ones digit. 5 or more rounds up.","explain":"{a} rounds to {=a-a%10+10*(a%10>=5)}. Check the ones digit: 5 or more rounds up, 4 or less rounds down.","params":{"a":[21,98]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Round {a} to the nearest 100.","answer":"a-a%100+100*(a%100>=50)","hint":"Look at the tens digit, and round in one step.","explain":"{a} rounds to {=a-a%100+100*(a%100>=50)}. Only the tens digit decides; do not round to the nearest 10 first.","params":{"a":[110,990]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Round 349 to the nearest 100.","choices":["300","350","400"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is 349 above or below the halfway point, 350?","explain":"349 is 49 from 300 and 51 from 400, so it rounds to 300. Rounding to 350 first and then up to 400 is a common slip."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Round 4,738 to the nearest 1000.","choices":["4,000","4,700","5,000"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the hundreds digit.","explain":"The hundreds digit is 7, which is 5 or more, so 4,738 rounds up to 5,000. 4,700 is the nearest 100, not the nearest 1000."}],"explain":{"kid":["Rounding gives a simpler number that is close to the real one. 37 is between 30 and 40. It is 7 away from 30 and only 3 away from 40. So 37 rounded to the nearest 10 is 40.","You do not need to count the gaps every time. Look at the digit just to the right of the place you are rounding to. If it is 5 or more, round up. If it is 4 or less, round down.","To round 4,367 to the nearest 100, look at the tens digit. It is 6, so round up to 4,400. To the nearest 1000, look at the hundreds digit. It is 3, so round down to 4,000.","A number exactly halfway, like 65 or 250, rounds up. And always round in one step. 349 is nearer 300 than 400, even though 349 rounds to 350 first."],"adult":["Ages 8 to 9. This matches CCSS 3.NBT.1 (use place value understanding to round whole numbers to the nearest 10 or 100) and Year 4 Number and place value in England (round any number to the nearest 10, 100 or 1000). It builds on Place value of each digit and leads into estimating and checking calculations.","Rounding to the nearest unit means choosing the multiple of that unit at the smallest distance. The digit test, 5 or more rounds up, is a shortcut for that distance comparison. A number exactly halfway has no nearer multiple, so the school rule is to round it up. The number line keeps the meaning of nearest in view.","Common mistakes: keeping only the first digit, so 37 becomes 30. Rounding in two steps, so 349 goes to 350 and then 400. Looking at the wrong digit, such as the ones digit when rounding to 100. Giving the nearest 10 when asked for the nearest 100. And rounding 65 down because it feels like it should go either way.","In the lab your child drags a marker along a number line and sees the distance to each end, finds the halfway case, rounds to the nearest 100 and 1000, rounds 4,367 three ways with the deciding digit lit, and estimates 389 + 512 as 400 + 500."]},"familyTip":"In a shop or on a car trip, pick a price or a distance and take turns rounding it to the nearest 10 and the nearest 100. Ask which two numbers it sits between, and which is nearer.","keyIdea":"To round a number, find the two multiples it sits between and choose the nearer one. Look at the digit just right of the place you round to: 5 or more rounds up, 4 or less rounds down. So 4,367 is 4,370 to the nearest 10, 4,400 to the nearest 100 and 4,000 to the nearest 1000.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Rounded to the nearest 100, is it 300 or 400? Drag each number into a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["rounds to 300","rounds to 400"],"items":[["349",0],["351",1],["310",0],["350",1],["298",0],["399",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagroundnl","caption":"Flagship lab: drag a marker along number lines to the nearer 10, 100 or 1000, meet the halfway rule, round one number three ways and estimate a sum.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_EDgw64OmfA":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"{a} × 100 = ?","answer":"a*100","hint":"Digits move two places left.","explain":"{a} × 100 = {=a*100}.","params":{"a":[2,99]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"{a} ÷ 100 = ?","answer":"a/100","hint":"Digits move two places right.","explain":"{a} ÷ 100 = {=a/100}.","params":{"a":[3,999]},"where":"a%10!=0"},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"In 0.35, what is the 3 worth?","choices":["3 tenths","3 ones","3 hundredths"],"answer":0,"hint":"The first place after the point.","explain":"The first place after the point is tenths."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"{=a/100} × 10 = ?","answer":"a/10","hint":"One place left.","explain":"{=a/100} × 10 = {=a/10}.","params":{"a":[11,99]},"where":"a%10!=0"},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A digit moves from the tens place to the hundreds place. What happened?","choices":["It was multiplied by 10","It was divided by 10","10 was added"],"answer":0,"hint":"Moving left makes it bigger.","explain":"One place left means ten times as much."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What is 6 ÷ 100?","choices":["0.06","0.6","600"],"answer":0,"hint":"Two places right.","explain":"6 ones become 6 hundredths: 0.06."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A digit in the hundreds place is worth how many times the same digit in the tens place?","choices":["10 times","100 times","One tenth"],"answer":0,"hint":"Next place to the left.","explain":"Each place is worth 10 times the one to its right."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Sam says 4.7 × 10 = 4.70 because “× 10 adds a zero”. What is the right answer?","choices":["47","4.70","4.07","470"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does 4.70 have a different value from 4.7?","explain":"4.70 is the same number as 4.7. × 10 moves each digit one place left: 47."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"{=a/10} × 10 = ?","answer":"a","hint":"Every digit moves one place to the left.","explain":"{=a/10} × 10 = {a}.","params":{"a":[11,99]},"where":"a%10!=0"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"{a} ÷ 10 = ?","answer":"a/10","hint":"Every digit moves one place to the right.","explain":"{a} ÷ 10 = {=a/10}.","params":{"a":[11,999]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"In 352, what is the 5 worth?","choices":["50","5","500"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which column is it in?","explain":"The 5 is in the tens column, so it is worth 50."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"What is 4.7 × 10?","choices":["47","4.70","470"],"answer":0,"hint":"The digits move one place left.","explain":"4 ones become 4 tens; 7 tenths become 7 ones: 47."}],"explain":{"kid":["Each place is worth ten times the place on its right. A ten is ten ones. A one is ten tenths.","When you multiply by 10, every digit moves one place to the left. When you divide by 10, every digit moves one place to the right. The decimal point stays still. A zero holds an empty place."],"adult":["Apples cost £0.35 each, so a crate of 10 costs £3.50 and a pallet of 100 costs £35. Multiplying by 10 moves every digit one place left; the decimal point does not move.","× 10 shifts each digit one column left: 4.7 × 10 = 47 and 4.7 × 100 = 470, with a zero as a placeholder. ÷ 10 shifts right: 47 ÷ 10 = 4.7 and 3 ÷ 100 = 0.03.","'Add a zero' fails for decimals: 4.7 × 10 is not 4.70. 'The digits move and the point stays' always works."]},"familyTip":"Write a number on paper strips, one digit per square, under a fixed decimal point. Slide the strip to multiply or divide by 10.","keyIdea":"Each place is worth ten times the place to its right, so × 10 moves every digit one place left and ÷ 10 moves it one place right.","visual":{"type":"placevalue","caption":"Press × 10 or ÷ 10 and watch the digits slide. The decimal point stays put.","opts":{"n":4.7}},"lab":{"type":"flagplace","caption":"Flagship lab: the apple stall. Watch the digits glide between columns while the decimal point stays put.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_AQcVRBddko":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which is less: 98 or 120?","choices":["98","120","They are equal"],"answer":0,"hint":"98 has no hundreds digit. Line up the ones before you compare.","explain":"98 has 0 hundreds and 120 has 1 hundred, so 98 < 120."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which symbol fits: 726 ? 726","choices":["=",">","<"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check every place: hundreds, tens and ones.","explain":"Every place matches (7 hundreds, 2 tens, 6 ones), so 726 = 726."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which symbol fits: 708 ? 780","choices":["<",">","="],"answer":0,"hint":"Same hundreds, so the tens decide. The open side faces the greater number.","explain":"Both have 7 hundreds. 0 tens is less than 8 tens, so 708 < 780."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Sam says 398 > 412 because 9 and 8 are big digits. What went wrong?","choices":["The hundreds decide first","He used the wrong symbol only","The ones decide first"],"answer":0,"hint":"Scan from the left. Which place differs first?","explain":"3 hundreds is less than 4 hundreds, so 398 < 412. The tens and ones never get a say."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"What is 100 more than {=h*100+t*10+o}?","answer":"h*100+t*10+o+100","hint":"100 more means the hundreds digit goes up by one. Tens and ones stay the same.","explain":"{=h*100+t*10+o} plus one hundred is {=h*100+t*10+o+100}.","params":{"h":[1,8],"t":[0,9],"o":[0,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which is greatest: 563, 536 or 365?","choices":["563","536","365"],"answer":0,"hint":"Two numbers have 5 hundreds. Compare their tens.","explain":"563 and 536 both have 5 hundreds. 6 tens beat 3 tens, so 563 is greatest."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"What is the tens digit of {=h*100+t*10+o}?","answer":"t","hint":"The tens digit is the middle digit of a three-digit number.","explain":"{=h*100+t*10+o} has {t} tens, so the tens digit is {t}.","params":{"h":[1,9],"t":[0,9],"o":[0,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Are 305 and 350 equal because they use the same digits?","choices":["No: 305 has 0 tens, 350 has 5 tens","Yes: same digits means equal","Yes: both have 3 hundreds"],"answer":0,"hint":"Equal needs every place to match, not just the same digits.","explain":"The tens differ (0 and 5), so 305 < 350. Same digits in different places give different numbers."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which is greater: 412 or 398?","choices":["412","398","They are equal"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check the hundreds digit of each number before anything else.","explain":"412 has 4 hundreds and 398 has 3 hundreds. More hundreds wins, so 412 > 398."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which symbol fits: 305 ? 350","choices":["<",">","="],"answer":0,"hint":"The hundreds match, so compare the tens. The zero means no tens.","explain":"Both have 3 hundreds. 0 tens is less than 5 tens, so 305 < 350."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"What number has {h} hundreds, {t} tens and {o} ones?","answer":"h*100+t*10+o","hint":"Each hundred is worth 100 and each ten is worth 10. Add the ones last.","explain":"{h} hundreds, {t} tens and {o} ones make {=h*100+t*10+o}.","params":{"h":[1,9],"t":[0,9],"o":[0,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Order 640, 604, 460 and 406. Which comes first (smallest)?","choices":["406","460","604","640"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the numbers with the fewest hundreds, then compare their tens.","explain":"406 and 460 have 4 hundreds. 0 tens is less than 6 tens, so 406 is smallest."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"What is the hundreds digit of {=h*100+t*10+o}?","answer":"h","hint":"In a three-digit number the hundreds digit is the one on the left.","explain":"{=h*100+t*10+o} is {h} hundreds, {t} tens and {o} ones, so the hundreds digit is {h}.","params":{"h":[1,9],"t":[0,9],"o":[0,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A three-digit number has hundreds, tens and ones. In 412 the 4 means 4 hundreds.","Start on the left. If one number has more hundreds, it is the bigger one. You can stop there. 412 is bigger than 398, even though 9 and 8 look big.","If the hundreds are the same, they do not help. Move right and look at the tens. Then the ones if you need to.","A zero still holds a place. 305 has no tens, but 350 has 5 tens. So 305 is less than 350. 98 has no hundreds at all. Put its ones under the ones of 120 first. Then you can see 98 is less."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 8. CCSS 2.NBT.4: compare two three-digit numbers based on meanings of the hundreds, tens and ones digits, using >, = and < symbols to record the results. National Curriculum Year 3 number and place value: compare and order numbers up to 1000.","The method is a left-to-right scan of aligned place-value columns. The first column where the digits differ decides the comparison, and every column to its right is irrelevant: 412 > 398 because 4 hundreds > 3 hundreds, regardless of 98 > 12. Ordering a set repeats the scan, grouping by hundreds and then by tens.","Common mistakes: letting the larger tens or ones digits win (calling 398 bigger than 412); treating numbers with the same digits as equal (305 and 350); ignoring a zero that holds a place; comparing the first digits of a two-digit and a three-digit number without aligning the places (98 vs 120, '9 > 1'); and turning the > or < sign the wrong way.","In the lab your child taps the column that decides on a chart of hundreds flats, tens rods and ones cubes, drags a symbol into the gap between 708 and 780, then drags 640, 604, 460 and 406 into order and sees them land on a 0 to 1000 number line."]},"familyTip":"Write two house numbers or page numbers one above the other, ones under ones. Ask which place to check first, then which place decides. Try a tricky pair like 305 and 350, or 98 and 120.","keyIdea":"To compare two numbers up to 1000, line up the places and scan from the left: hundreds first, then tens, then ones. The first place where the digits differ decides. Record it with >, = or <, and order a set by doing the same scan.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which symbol fits each pair? Drag the pair into >, = or <, or tap the pair then a box.","opts":{"bins":[">","=","<"],"items":[["412 ? 398",0],["305 ? 350",2],["726 ? 726",1],["98 ? 120",2],["640 ? 604",0],["815 ? 851",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagord1k","caption":"Flagship lab: tap the place that decides on a hundreds, tens and ones chart, drag a symbol into the gap, then order four numbers onto a 0 to 1000 line.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_9L3NQqgqRd":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"2 hundreds, no tens and 5 ones is…","choices":["205","25","250"],"answer":0,"hint":"There are hundreds, so the number needs three digits. What goes in the empty tens place?","explain":"Write 2 for the hundreds, 0 for no tens and 5 for the ones: 205."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"{=100*h+10*t+o} is between two hundreds on a number line. What is the smaller one?","answer":"100*h","hint":"Look at the hundreds digit.","explain":"{=100*h+10*t+o} has {h} hundreds, so it is between {=100*h} and {=100*h+100}. The smaller one is {=100*h}.","params":{"h":[1,8],"t":[1,9],"o":[0,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"On a number line from 0 to 1000, where does 346 go?","choices":["A bit before the middle","Near the end","Next to 34"],"answer":0,"hint":"The middle of the line is 500. Is 346 more or less?","explain":"346 is less than 500, between 300 and 400, so it goes a bit before the middle."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A number line goes from 0 to 100. A flag is half way between 0 and 50. What number is it at?","answer":25,"hint":"Half way between 0 and 50 is half of 50.","explain":"Half way between 0 and 50 is half of 50, which is 25."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"{h} hundreds and {o} ones, with no tens. What number is that?","answer":"100*h+o","hint":"Put a 0 in the tens place.","explain":"{h} for the hundreds, 0 for no tens and {o} for the ones: {=100*h+o}.","params":{"h":[1,9],"o":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What does it mean to estimate how many beads are in a jar?","choices":["Make a sensible close guess","Count every bead","Say any number"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the ring of 10 in the lab.","explain":"An estimate is a close, sensible number. You use a group you know, like 10, to judge the rest without counting them all."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which one is NOT the same as 46?","choices":["4 ones and 6 tens","4 tens and 6 ones","40 + 6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which digit is in the tens place in 46?","explain":"46 is 4 tens and 6 ones, which is 40 + 6. 4 ones and 6 tens is 64, a different number."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Where does 9 go on a number line from 0 to 100?","choices":["Near 0, just before 10","Near the middle","Near 100, because 9 is a big digit"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is 9 more or less than 10?","explain":"9 is less than 10, so it goes near 0, just before the first ten. 9 is big for a digit but small on this line."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Where does 52 go on a number line from 0 to 100?","choices":["Just past the middle","Near 0","Near 100"],"answer":0,"hint":"The middle of a 0 to 100 line is 50. Is 52 a bit more or a bit less?","explain":"52 is just more than 50, so it goes just past the middle."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"{h} hundreds, {t} tens and {o} ones make what number?","answer":"100*h+10*t+o","hint":"Write the hundreds digit first, then the tens, then the ones. Use 0 for an empty place.","explain":"{h} hundreds is {=100*h}, {t} tens is {=10*t} and {o} ones is {o}. Together they make {=100*h+10*t+o}.","params":{"h":[1,9],"t":[0,9],"o":[0,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A number line goes from 0 to {=2*m}. What number is in the middle?","answer":"m","hint":"The middle is half way. What is half of the end number?","explain":"Half of {=2*m} is {m}, so {m} is in the middle of the line.","params":{"m":[5,50]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A ring of 10 marbles fills about a third of a bag. What is the best estimate for the whole bag?","choices":["About 30","About 10","About 100"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many rings of 10 would fill the bag?","explain":"About 3 groups of 10 fill the bag, so there are about 30 marbles."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"What is the tens digit in {=100*h+10*t+o} worth?","answer":"10*t","hint":"Find the middle digit of the three. It counts tens.","explain":"In {=100*h+10*t+o} the middle digit, {t}, is in the tens place, so it is worth {t} tens, which is {=10*t}.","params":{"h":[1,9],"t":[1,9],"o":[0,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["You can show a number in lots of ways. You can write it: 346. You can build it with blocks: 3 hundreds, 4 tens and 6 ones. You can mark it on a number line. They all show the same number. If a place is empty, write 0 there. 2 hundreds and 5 ones is 205, not 25.","A number line has the numbers in order, with equal gaps. Look at the start, the middle and the end. On a line from 0 to 100, the middle is 50. 37 is a bit less than 50, so it goes a bit before the middle.","On a line from 0 to 1000, the middle is 500. 346 is between 300 and 400, so it goes a bit before the middle too. A number with three digits does not always go near the end.","To estimate means to make a good, close guess. Do not count every bead. Look at a group of 10. If about 4 groups like that fill the jar, there are about 40 beads."],"adult":["Ages 6 to 8. NC Year 2 asks children to identify, represent and estimate numbers using different representations, including the number line. Year 3 takes this up to 1000. Key words: numeral, digit, place value, hundreds, tens, ones, base-ten blocks (flats, rods and cubes), number line, halfway and estimate.","Common mistakes: treating a number line as a row of labels, not a scale, so 37 goes near 0 because it feels small. Thinking any three-digit number goes near the end of a 0 to 1000 line. Writing 2 hundreds and 5 ones as 25, with no 0 to hold the empty tens place, or mixing up the tens and ones. Thinking an estimate is any guess, or that you must count to get it.","Placing numbers on a line is a good sign of number sense. Children who place numbers well on a line tend to do better at sums later (Siegler and Booth, 2004). Benchmarks help: find the middle first, then the tens or hundreds on either side. For amounts, a known group, here a ring of 10, is a benchmark too.","In the lab your child places 37 on a 0 to 100 line and 346 on a 0 to 1000 line, using the middle mark. They estimate a jar of 42 beads from a ring of 10, then count to check. They build 346 from flats, rods and cubes while a pointer moves along a 0 to 1000 line. Last, they match blocks to numbers like 205, 170 and 403."]},"familyTip":"Chalk a long line on the path, or draw one on paper. Write 0 at one end and 100 at the other, and ask where 50 goes. Then call out numbers and let your child stand or point where each one goes. To practise estimating, pour pasta into a bowl, make one pile of 10, and guess the total before you count.","keyIdea":"One number can be shown in different ways: as a numeral (346), as base-ten blocks (3 hundreds, 4 tens and 6 ones) and as a point on a number line. When a place is empty, a 0 holds it: 2 hundreds and 5 ones is 205. To place a number on a line, use the start, the middle and the end: on a 0 to 100 line, 37 goes a bit before 50. To estimate how many things there are, look at a group of 10 and judge how many groups fit. An estimate is a sensible close number, not a wild guess.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"On a 0 to 100 line, is each number near 0, near the middle (50) or near 100? Drag each card to a box, or tap the card then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Near 0","Near 50","Near 100"],"items":[["4",0],["9",0],["46",1],["53",1],["91",2],["97",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagnline","caption":"Flagship lab: place 37 and 346 on number lines, estimate a jar of beads, build 346 with blocks and match blocks to numbers.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_RVK655t391":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"It warms from −{a} °C to −{b} °C. How many degrees did it rise?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Both are below zero.","explain":"From −{a} up to −{b} is {=a-b} degrees.","params":{"a":[5,15],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A freezer is at −{a} °C and the kitchen is at {b} °C. How many degrees warmer is the kitchen?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Count through zero.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} degrees warmer.","params":{"a":[10,25],"b":[15,25]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A diver is at −20 m. A bird is at 15 m. How far apart are they?","choices":["35 m","5 m","−5 m"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count through sea level (0).","explain":"20 + 15 = 35 m."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which is warmest?","choices":["−1 °C","−10 °C","−4 °C"],"answer":0,"hint":"Closest to zero.","explain":"−1 is the closest to zero, so it is warmest."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Overnight it falls {d} degrees from {a} °C and ends below zero. How many degrees below zero is it?","answer":"d-a","hint":"First {a} degrees take it to zero.","explain":"It ends at −{=d-a} °C, which is {=d-a} below zero.","params":{"a":[1,9],"d":[10,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What is 3 − 8?","choices":["−5","5","−11"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start at 3 and go down 8.","explain":"3 down to 0, then 5 more: −5."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A bank balance is −£{a}. You pay in £{b}. What is the balance now, in £?","answer":"b-a","hint":"First £{a} brings it to zero.","explain":"−{a} + {b} = £{=b-a}.","params":{"a":[5,40],"b":[50,90]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"It is −{a} °C at night and {b} °C at noon. How many degrees did it rise?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Count up to zero, then on.","explain":"{a} up to zero, then {b} more: {=a+b} degrees.","params":{"a":[1,10],"b":[1,15]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"How many degrees is the drop from {b} °C to −{a} °C?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Down to zero, then below.","explain":"{b} down to zero, then {a} more: {=a+b} degrees.","params":{"a":[1,10],"b":[1,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which is coldest?","choices":["−8 °C","−3 °C","0 °C"],"answer":0,"hint":"Further below zero is colder.","explain":"−8 is the furthest below zero."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which order goes from coldest to warmest?","choices":["−7, −2, 0, 4","0, −2, −7, 4","4, 0, −2, −7"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start with the most negative.","explain":"−7 < −2 < 0 < 4."}],"explain":{"kid":["Temperatures below zero are negative. −5 °C is five degrees colder than 0 °C.","To find a change across zero, count to zero first, then count on. From −5 °C to 8 °C is 5 up to zero, then 8 more. That is a rise of 13 degrees."],"adult":["Negative numbers measure below a zero point: temperature, money owed, depth below sea level. The bigger the number after the minus sign, the lower the value: −12 is colder than −3.","Difference = higher − lower, even with negatives: 8 − (−5) = 13. A number line or a thermometer makes the step through zero easy to see."]},"familyTip":"Check the weather forecast for a cold city. Find the difference between the night and day temperatures.","keyIdea":"Below zero is negative; to find a change across zero, count to zero first and then on.","visual":{"type":"thermo","caption":"The dashed line is 8 °C. Drag the thermometer, or press Colder and Warmer, and read the change across zero.","opts":{"min":-20,"max":40,"t":-5,"from":8,"step":1}},"lab":{"type":"flagneg","caption":"Flagship lab: the mountain hut. Drag the thermometer through zero and count every degree.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_lC_Q5mSL_I":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is 3,900 + 100?","choices":["4,000","3,1000","3,910"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ten 100s make 1,000. What happens when the hundreds place is already 9?","explain":"3,900 already has 9 hundreds. One more hundred makes 10 hundreds, which is 1,000, so the number becomes 4,000."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A card shows 8 thousands counters and 2 tens counters. What number is it?","choices":["8,020","8,200","820"],"answer":0,"hint":"Read the labels on the counters. Are the 2 counters hundreds or tens?","explain":"8 thousands and 2 tens, with no hundreds and no ones, is 8,020. 8,200 would need 2 hundreds counters."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"How do you write {=n*1000} + 50 as a four-digit numeral?","answer":"n*1000+50","hint":"Fifty is 5 tens. The hundreds place is empty, so write a 0 there.","explain":"{=n*1000} + 50 is {n} thousands, 0 hundreds, 5 tens and 0 ones: {=n*1000+50}.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"How many hundreds are there in 1,000?","choices":["10","100","1,000"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each hundred is 100. How many lots of 100 make 1,000?","explain":"10 × 100 = 1,000, so ten hundreds make 1,000. 100 hundreds would be 10,000."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Kai says 4,090 is near the end of a line from 4,000 to 5,000. What is wrong?","choices":["4,090 has 0 hundreds, so it is just after 4,000","Nothing, 4,090 is nearly 5,000","4,090 is halfway, at 4,500"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the hundreds digit. Is it 0 or 9?","explain":"4,090 has 0 hundreds and 9 tens, so it sits just before 4,100, near the start of a 4,000 to 5,000 line."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"{n} thousands counters and 3 hundreds counters show which number?","answer":"n*1000+300","hint":"Write the thousands, then the hundreds, then 0 tens and 0 ones.","explain":"{n} thousands and 3 hundreds is {=n*1000+300}.","params":{"n":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which expanded form equals 6,407?","choices":["6000 + 400 + 7","6000 + 40 + 7","6000 + 400 + 70"],"answer":0,"hint":"The 4 is in the hundreds place and the 7 is in the ones place.","explain":"6,407 is 6 thousands, 4 hundreds, 0 tens and 7 ones, so 6000 + 400 + 7."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"What is 7,990 + 10?","choices":["8,000","7,1000","8,090"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ten tens make 100, and ten 100s make 1,000. Watch both places fill up.","explain":"7,990 + 10 makes 10 tens, which is 1 hundred, and that makes 10 hundreds, so the number becomes 8,000."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"On a number line from 0 to 10,000, which number sits about halfway?","choices":["5,000","500","1,000"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each big tick is 1,000. How many ticks take you halfway from 0 to 10,000?","explain":"Halfway from 0 to 10,000 is 5,000. 500 and 1,000 are both near the start of the line."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A number has {n} thousands, 4 hundreds, 0 tens and 7 ones. What is the number?","answer":"n*1000+407","hint":"Put the thousands digit in the thousands place, then write 407 after it.","explain":"{n} thousands is {=n*1000}, then 4 hundreds, 0 tens and 7 ones make {=n*1000+407}.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"You start at {=n*1000} and count on 100 three times. Where do you land?","answer":"n*1000+300","hint":"Each hop adds 100. Three hops add 300.","explain":"Three hops of 100 add 300, so {=n*1000} + 300 = {=n*1000+300}.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"How do you write three thousand and fifty as a numeral?","choices":["3,050","3,500","3,005"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fifty is 5 tens. The hundreds place is empty, so a 0 holds it.","explain":"Three thousand and fifty is 3 thousands, 0 hundreds, 5 tens and 0 ones: 3,050. 3,500 is three thousand, five hundred."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Where does 6,400 sit on a 0 to 10,000 number line?","choices":["Between 6,000 and 7,000, a little less than halfway on","Near 640, close to the start","Past 7,000"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the thousands first. How many thousands does 6,400 have?","explain":"6,400 has 6 thousands, so it sits between 6,000 and 7,000. 400 is less than halfway to the next thousand."}],"explain":{"kid":["A number up to 10,000 can have four digits. From the left they are thousands, hundreds, tens and ones. 6,407 has 6 thousands, 4 hundreds, 0 tens and 7 ones.","Draw a line from 0 to 10,000. Each big tick is 1,000 more. Halfway is 5,000, not 500. To place 6,400, go past 6,000, then a bit less than halfway towards 7,000.","The same number can be written in more than one way. In words it is six thousand, four hundred and seven. As a sum it is 6000 + 400 + 7. A 0 holds a place that is empty, so three thousand and fifty is 3,050, not 3,500.","Ten of one place make one of the next. Ten 100s make 1,000, so 3,900 + 100 is 4,000, not 3,1000."],"adult":["Ages 8 to 9. In England, Year 4 number and place value asks pupils to recognise the place value of each digit in a four-digit number, to identify, represent and estimate numbers using different representations, and to count in multiples of 6, 7, 9, 25 and 1,000, including finding 1,000 more or less. The statutory guidance also includes reading and writing numbers to at least 10,000, and the related objective of rounding. US Common Core 4.NBT.A.2 asks pupils to read and write multi-digit whole numbers in standard form, word form and expanded form, and to compare them.","The new idea, beyond knowing what each digit is worth, is to represent and estimate the same four-digit number in several ways: on a 0–10,000 number line (and on a zoomed 4,000–5,000 line whose ticks are 100), in words, in expanded form, and with place value counters. Halfway from 0 to 10,000 is 5,000. Ten hundreds make one thousand, so counting on can cross a thousands boundary.","Common mistakes: thinking 500 or 1,000 is halfway to 10,000; placing 6,400 near 640 because the digits look the same; writing three thousand and fifty as 3,500, or 3,1000 when adding 100 to 3,900; and treating 10 hundreds as 10,000. In the lab an arrow on the wrong tick, a wrong numeral card, or a count that will not carry shows the slip."]},"familyTip":"Ask your child to mark a house number or a price, such as 6,400, on a 0 to 10,000 number line you draw together. Then say the same number in words and as a sum of thousands, hundreds, tens and ones.","keyIdea":"Numbers up to 10,000 have four places: thousands, hundreds, tens and ones. On a 0 to 10,000 number line, each big tick is 1,000 more, so 5,000 is halfway. To place a number such as 6,400, find the thousands either side first, then go part of the way on. The same number can be shown as a numeral, in words, as a sum of its parts or with place value counters; a 0 holds an empty place, so three thousand and fifty is 3,050, not 3,500. Ten of one place make one of the next: 3,900 + 100 is 4,000, not 3,1000.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does this match 6,407? Drag each card to a box, or tap the card and then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Yes, it is 6,407","No, a different number"],"items":[["six thousand, four hundred and seven",0],["6000 + 400 + 7",0],["6 thousands, 4 hundreds, 7 ones",0],["six thousand and forty-seven",1],["6000 + 40 + 7",1],["6,470",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flag10k","caption":"Flagship lab: put thousands on a 0–10,000 line, estimate 6,400, zoom into 4,000–5,000, match words, sums and counters, and count on past a thousand.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_MHaiUd2FLA":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which list is in order, smallest first?","choices":["3,099, 3,909, 3,990","3,990, 3,909, 3,099","3,909, 3,099, 3,990"],"answer":0,"hint":"All have 3 thousands. Compare the hundreds next.","explain":"Hundreds: 0, 9, 9. So 3,099 is smallest. 3,909 and 3,990 tie in the hundreds; tens 0 < 9, so 3,909 comes next."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Ben says 999 is bigger than 1,001 because it has bigger digits. Is he right?","choices":["No: 999 has 0 thousands, 1,001 has 1","Yes: 9 is more than 1","Yes: 999 has more digits that are big"],"answer":0,"hint":"Line up the ones. How many thousands does 999 have?","explain":"999 has no thousands digit, so it has 0 thousands. 1,001 has 1 thousand, so it is bigger."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Use each of the digits 1, {d}, 3 and 0 once. What is the biggest number you can make? Type it without commas.","answer":"d*1000+310","hint":"Put the biggest digit in the thousands, then the next biggest.","explain":"{d} is the biggest digit, then 3, 1 and 0: {d}310.","params":{"d":[4,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"5,2□8 > 5,268. Which digit could go in the box?","choices":["7","6","4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Thousands and hundreds tie. The tens must beat 6, or tie and win on the ones.","explain":"5,278 > 5,268 because tens 7 > 6. 6 gives 5,268, which is equal, not greater. 4 gives a smaller number."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is the largest?","choices":["8,010","7,999","8,001"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the thousands first, then go right.","explain":"7,999 has only 7 thousands. 8,010 and 8,001 tie in thousands and hundreds; tens 1 > 0, so 8,010 is largest."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Which is bigger: {a}0{b}5 or {a}{b}05? Type the bigger number.","answer":"a*1000+b*100+5","hint":"The thousands tie. Look at the hundreds: 0 or {b}?","explain":"{a}{b}05 has {b} hundreds and {a}0{b}5 has 0 hundreds, so {a}{b}05 is bigger.","params":{"a":[1,9],"b":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which sentence is true?","choices":["4,560 < 4,605","4,560 > 4,605","4,560 = 4,605"],"answer":0,"hint":"Same digits, different places. Compare the hundreds.","explain":"Thousands tie. Hundreds: 5 < 6, so 4,560 < 4,605. The open side faces the bigger number."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why is 2,001 bigger than 1,999?","choices":["It has more thousands","It ends in 1","Its digits add up to more"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which column do you check first?","explain":"Compare from the left. 2 thousands > 1 thousand, so 2,001 is bigger. The other digits do not matter after that."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which is bigger: 6,048 or 6,084?","choices":["6,084","6,048","They are equal"],"answer":0,"hint":"Line them up. The thousands tie, and the hundreds tie. Which place differs next?","explain":"Both have 6 thousands and 0 hundreds. Tens: 8 > 4, so 6,084 is bigger."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Which is bigger: {a}999 or {=a+1}000? Type the bigger number without commas.","answer":"(a+1)*1000","hint":"The thousands digits are different. Which one has more thousands?","explain":"{=a+1} thousands is more than {a} thousands, so {=a+1}000 is bigger, even with all those 9s in {a}999.","params":{"a":[1,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which sign makes 7,305 □ 7,350 true?","choices":["<",">","="],"answer":0,"hint":"Thousands tie at 7, hundreds tie at 3. Look at the tens.","explain":"Tens: 0 < 5, so 7,305 < 7,350. The open side faces 7,350, the bigger number."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A number has {t} thousands, 4 hundreds, 0 tens and 9 ones. Another has {t} thousands, 3 hundreds, 9 tens and 9 ones. Type the bigger number.","answer":"t*1000+409","hint":"The thousands are the same. Compare the hundreds.","explain":"Both have {t} thousands. 4 hundreds > 3 hundreds, so {t}409 is bigger than {t}399.","params":{"t":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Use each of 2, 9, 0 and 6 once. What is the smallest 4-digit number?","choices":["2,069","0,269","2,096"],"answer":0,"hint":"0 cannot go first. Which is the smallest digit that is not 0?","explain":"2 goes first, because 0 first would give a 3-digit number. Then 0, 6 and 9 in order: 2,069."}],"explain":{"kid":["Which is bigger, 4,321 or 4,312? Write them one above the other so the ones are lined up. Start on the left. Both have 4 thousands. Both have 3 hundreds. The tens are 2 and 1. 2 tens beat 1 ten, so 4,321 is bigger. You can stop there. The ones do not matter now.","Lots of big digits do not win. 1,999 is smaller than 2,001, because 1 thousand is less than 2 thousands. A number with fewer digits has no thousands at all. So 987 is smaller than 1,203, even though 9 is more than 1.","To put numbers in order, find the smallest first. Then find the next smallest, and keep going. We write 4,312 < 4,321. The sign < means less than and > means greater than. The open side of the sign faces the bigger number.","Try this with the cards 3, 0, 7 and 5. For the biggest number, put the biggest digit first: 7,530. For the smallest, you might want 0 first. But 0,357 is just 357, which has only 3 digits. So put 3 first, then 0: 3,057."],"adult":["Ages 8 to 9. The National Curriculum in England asks Year 4 pupils to order and compare numbers beyond 1000, and Year 5 extends this to 1,000,000. The method is the same at every size. Line up the place value columns and compare from the highest place. The first column that differs decides. CCSS 2.NBT.4 and 4.NBT.2 use the same reasoning with the symbols >, = and <.","The lab builds this step by step. Pupils decide pairs where only one column differs. They slide a 3-digit number so ones sit under ones. Then they order four numbers that tie in the thousands and hundreds. They build the biggest and smallest numbers from four digit cards, one of them 0. Last, they find every digit that makes 4,□52 > 4,751 true. With 7 the hundreds tie, so the tens and then the ones decide, which many pupils miss.","Common mistakes. Pupils compare the ones first or add up the digits. They line up numbers by their first digit, so 987 looks bigger than 1,203. They think more 9s make a bigger number, or put 0 first when making the smallest number. They reverse < and >, or think a tie in one column means the numbers are equal. Each of these slips gets its own message in the lab and the challenge."]},"familyTip":"Read out two house numbers, prices or car mileages you see. Your child says which is bigger and which column decided it. Then swap roles: you make a pair that ties in the thousands and hundreds, and they find the place that breaks the tie.","keyIdea":"To compare numbers beyond 1000, line them up so ones sit under ones, then compare place by place from the left. The first place value column where the digits differ decides, and the digits after it do not matter: 1,999 < 2,001 because 1 thousand < 2 thousands. A number with fewer digits has 0 in the bigger places, so 987 < 1,203. To order a set, find the smallest, then the next smallest. With digit cards, put the biggest digit in the thousands for the biggest number; for the smallest, 0 cannot go first, so 3, 0, 7, 5 makes 3,057. Write the answer with < (less than) or > (greater than); the open side faces the bigger number.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is the first number bigger or smaller? Drag each pair to a box, or tap it and then a box.","opts":{"bins":["First is bigger (>)","First is smaller (<)"],"items":[["3,410 and 3,401",0],["7,002 and 6,999",0],["5,120 and 512",0],["2,089 and 2,098",1],["999 and 1,000",1],["8,506 and 8,560",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagbigcmp","caption":"Flagship lab: compare column by column, line up ones, order four numbers, build biggest and smallest, find a missing digit.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Gag_h98jWP":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which is 7,045,200 in words?","choices":["seven million, forty-five thousand, two hundred","seven million, four hundred and fifty thousand, two hundred","seventy million, forty-five thousand, two hundred"],"answer":0,"hint":"Read the middle group, 045, as one number.","explain":"7 is the millions group, 045 is forty-five thousand and 200 is two hundred: seven million, forty-five thousand, two hundred."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Mia writes four million and sixty as 4,60. What should it be?","choices":["4,000,060","4,060","4,600,000"],"answer":0,"hint":"Every group needs three digits, and the thousands group is empty.","explain":"The thousands group is empty, so it is 000, and sixty is 060 in the ones group: 4,000,060."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"What is the value of the {a} in {a},300,000? Type it without commas.","answer":"a*1000000","hint":"The first group of a seven-digit number is the millions.","explain":"The {a} is in the millions place, so it is worth {a} × 1,000,000 = {=a*1000000}.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which list is in order from smallest to largest?","choices":["456,000 < 4,506,000 < 4,560,000","4,560,000 < 4,506,000 < 456,000","4,506,000 < 456,000 < 4,560,000"],"answer":0,"hint":"456,000 has fewer digits than the others.","explain":"456,000 has no millions, so it is smallest. 4,506,000 and 4,560,000 have the same millions and hundred-thousands; 0 ten-thousands is less than 6, so 4,506,000 comes next."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Write {a} million, {b} hundred thousand and {c} as a number. Type it without commas.","answer":"a*1000000+b*100000+c","hint":"{b} hundred thousand is {b} in the hundred-thousands place.","explain":"{a} million, then {b} in the hundred-thousands place, zeros in the empty places, and {c} in the ones: {=a*1000000+b*100000+c}.","params":{"a":[1,9],"b":[1,9],"c":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Leo says 999,999 is bigger than 1,000,000 because it has more nines. Is he right?","choices":["No: 1,000,000 has more digits, so it is bigger","Yes: 9 is bigger than 1","They are equal"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the digits in each number.","explain":"1,000,000 has seven digits and 999,999 has six. A number with more digits is bigger, so 1,000,000 is larger by 1."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"In 3,333,333, what is the 3 in the ten-thousands place worth?","choices":["30,000","3,000","300,000"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count from the right: ones, tens, hundreds, thousands, ten-thousands.","explain":"The ten-thousands place is the fifth from the right, so that 3 is worth 3 × 10,000 = 30,000."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which is six million, fifty thousand and nine?","choices":["6,050,009","6,509","6,500,009"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fifty thousand fills the thousands group as 050.","explain":"6 millions, then 050 thousands, then 009 ones: 6,050,009. Leaving out zeros gives 6,509, which is far too small."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Where do the commas go in 5208416?","choices":["5,208,416","520,841,6","52,08,416"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start at the ones, on the right, and count three digits for each comma.","explain":"From the right: 416 is the ones group, 208 is the thousands group and 5 is the millions group, so it is 5,208,416."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"What is {a} million, {b} thousand and {c} written as a number? Type it without commas.","answer":"a*1000000+b*1000+c","hint":"Give each group three digits: {a} million, then the thousands group, then the ones group.","explain":"{a} million is {a}, then {b} thousand fills the thousands group and {c} fills the ones group. Together that is {=a*1000000+b*1000+c}.","params":{"a":[1,9],"b":[10,99],"c":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What is the value of the 8 in 2,830,415?","choices":["800,000","8","80,000"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which place is the 8 in? Count from the ones on the right.","explain":"The 8 is in the hundred-thousands place, so it is worth 8 × 100,000 = 800,000."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"How many thousands are in {a},000,000?","answer":"a*1000","hint":"One million is 1,000 thousands.","explain":"1,000,000 is 1,000 thousands, so {a},000,000 is {a} × 1,000 = {=a*1000} thousands.","params":{"a":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which number is the largest?","choices":["3,100,000","999,999","3,099,999"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the digits first, then compare from the left.","explain":"999,999 has no millions, so it is the smallest. 3,100,000 and 3,099,999 both have 3 millions; then 1 hundred-thousand beats 0, so 3,100,000 is the largest."}],"explain":{"kid":["A long number like 4302561 is hard to read in one go. So we split it into groups of three digits. Start at the right, at the ones, and count three digits. Put a comma. Count three more and put another comma. Now it is 4,302,561.","Each group has a name. The group on the right is the ones. The middle group is the thousands. The group on the left is the millions. To say the number, read each group as a number up to 999, then say its name: four million, three hundred and two thousand, five hundred and sixty-one.","To write a number from words, fill each group with three digits. Fifty thousand is the group 050. If a place has nothing in it, write a 0 there. So six million, fifty thousand and nine is 6,050,009. Without the zeros, the 5 and the 9 slide into the wrong places.","Each place is worth ten times the place to its right. In 3,333,333 every digit is a 3, but they are worth different amounts. The 3 in the ten-thousands place is worth 30,000. The 3 on the left is worth 3,000,000. To compare two numbers, count the digits first. 999,999 has six digits and no millions, so it is smaller than 1,000,000. If two numbers have the same number of digits, compare from the left. The first place that is different decides."],"adult":["Ages 10 to 11. The National Curriculum in England asks Year 6 pupils to read, write, order and compare numbers up to 10,000,000 and to determine the value of each digit. In the US, CCSS 4.NBT.A.2 covers, for numbers up to 1,000,000, reading and writing multi-digit numbers with base-ten numerals, number names and expanded form, and comparing them with >, = and <.","The lab works with the three groups (often called periods): ones, thousands and millions. Children tap commas into 4302561, 10000000 and 7045200, choose the words for each group of three numbers, build 6,050,009 and 3,400,070 from words on a seven-column chart, find a digit by its value in 3,333,333 and 5,050,505, and order 456,000, 4,506,000, 4,560,000 and 4,605,000.","Common mistakes. Children often count digits from the left when they place commas, or read each digit on its own instead of reading a group as one number. When writing from words they leave out zeros (writing 6,509 for six million, fifty thousand and nine) or put a zero in the wrong place. Many judge the size of a number by its first digit or by how many large digits it has, rather than by its number of digits and then by place. British English says 'and' inside a group (three hundred and two thousand); American English usually leaves it out. Both are correct."]},"familyTip":"Find big numbers in the news, on a football stadium or in a country's population. Take turns reading them aloud in groups of three, then cover one digit and ask what it is worth.","keyIdea":"Big numbers are read in groups of three digits, counted from the right: the ones group, the thousands group and the millions group. Commas mark the groups, so 4302561 is written 4,302,561. Read each group as a number from 0 to 999 and then say its name: four million, three hundred and two thousand, five hundred and sixty-one. To write a number from words, give every group three digits and hold empty places with 0: six million, fifty thousand and nine is 6,050,009. A digit's value is the digit times its place, so the 7 in 7,045,200 is worth 7,000,000. To compare, a number with more digits is bigger; with the same number of digits, the first place from the left that differs decides.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"What is the 5 worth in each number? Drag each card to a box, or tap it and then a box.","opts":{"bins":["5,000,000","50,000","500"],"items":[["5,210,000",0],["5,000,001",0],["5,999,999",0],["1,250,000",1],["9,056,300",1],["3,452,000",1],["2,100,500",2],["8,000,501",2],["6,743,598",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagmil","caption":"Flagship lab: comma groups of three, say each group, build numbers from words with zeros, find a digit's value, order seven-digit numbers.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_MewIRdzpzz":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What number is L?","choices":["50","100","5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the five tiles: I, V, X, L and C.","explain":"L is 50. C is 100 and V is 5."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What number is IX?","choices":["9","11","10"],"answer":0,"hint":"The small I is before the big X. Is it added or taken away?","explain":"IX is 10 take away 1, which is 9. XI, with the I after, is 11."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"How do you write 40?","choices":["XL","XXXX","LX"],"answer":0,"hint":"Four X's in a row is not allowed. Take 10 away from 50.","explain":"40 is XL: the X before the L takes 10 away from 50. LX is 60."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What number is XCIV?","choices":["94","116","96"],"answer":0,"hint":"Split it into two pairs: XC and IV. Work out each one.","explain":"XC is 90 and IV is 4, so XCIV is 94. Adding every symbol gives 116, which is wrong."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"How do you write 99?","choices":["XCIX","IC","LXXXXVIIII"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write 90 first, then 9. Which pairs make 90 and 9?","explain":"99 is 90 + 9, so XC then IX: XCIX. IC is not allowed, because I only goes before V or X."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which number has no Roman numeral?","choices":["0","50","100"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which number did the Romans never need to write?","explain":"There is no Roman numeral for zero. L is 50 and C is 100."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What number is LXXVIII?","choices":["78","73","58"],"answer":0,"hint":"L, then X's, then V, then I's. Add them all.","explain":"50 + 10 + 10 + 5 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 78. Nothing is taken away, because each symbol is the same size or smaller than the one before."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which numeral is written the right way?","choices":["XIX","XVIIII","IXX"],"answer":0,"hint":"Tens first, then ones. 19 is 10 and 9.","explain":"19 is X and IX: XIX. XVIIII has four I's in a row, and IXX puts the ones before the tens."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"How do you write 5?","choices":["V","VX","IIIII"],"answer":0,"hint":"There is a symbol of its own for 5.","explain":"5 is V. VX is not allowed: a V is never taken away."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m10","prompt":"A numeral is {a} X's, then {b} I's. What number is it?","answer":"10*a+b","params":{"a":[1,3],"b":[1,3]},"hint":"Each X is 10 and each I is 1. Count the tens, then the ones.","explain":"{a} tens and {b} ones: {=10*a+b}."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m11","prompt":"A numeral is L, then {a} X's, then V, then {b} I's. What number is it?","answer":"55+10*a+b","params":{"a":[1,3],"b":[1,3]},"hint":"Add 50, then 10 for each X, then 5, then 1 for each I.","explain":"50 + {a} × 10 + 5 + {b} = {=55+10*a+b}."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What number is XVI?","choices":["16","14","61"],"answer":0,"hint":"Small after big means add. Add 10, 5 and 1.","explain":"XVI is 10 + 5 + 1 = 16. The I comes after the V, so it is added."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A numeral is L, then {a} X's, then {b} I's. What number is it?","answer":"50+10*a+b","params":{"a":[1,3],"b":[1,3]},"hint":"L is 50, each X is 10 and each I is 1. Add them all.","explain":"50 + {a} × 10 + {b} = {=50+10*a+b}. Every symbol after the L is smaller, so they are all added."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"How do you write 4?","choices":["IV","IIII","VI"],"answer":0,"hint":"You may not write four I's. Which pair takes 1 away from 5?","explain":"4 is IV: the small I before the V takes 1 away from 5. VI is 6."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"One numeral is XL, then {a} I's. Another is XC, then {b} I's. What is the bigger number?","answer":"90+b","params":{"a":[1,3],"b":[1,3]},"hint":"XL is 50 take away 10. XC is 100 take away 10. Which is bigger?","explain":"XL is 40 and XC is 90, so the XC numeral is bigger: 90 + {b} = {=90+b}."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Sam writes 49 as IL. What should it be?","choices":["XLIX","XXXXIX","LIX"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write the tens, then the ones. What is 40? What is 9?","explain":"I can only go before V or X. 49 is 40 + 9, so XL then IX: XLIX."}],"explain":{"kid":["Romans wrote numbers with letters. I is 1, V is 5, X is 10, L is 50 and C is 100.","Put the biggest symbols first, then add them up. XVII is 10 + 5 + 1 + 1, which is 17.","You may write the same symbol three times in a row, but not four. So 4 is not IIII. Put a small I before the V: IV means 5 take away 1, which is 4.","Only four take-away pairs are needed up to 100: IV is 4, IX is 9, XL is 40 and XC is 90. Write the tens first, then the ones. So 49 is XL and IX: XLIX."],"adult":["Ages 8 to 9. The National Curriculum in England asks Year 4 pupils to read Roman numerals to 100 (I to C) and to know that over time the numeral system changed to include zero and place value. Year 3 starts with clock faces (I to XII). Values: I 1, V 5, X 10, L 50, C 100.","The modern rules: symbols are written from largest to smallest and added; a smaller symbol directly before a larger one is subtracted, using only the pairs IV, IX, XL, XC (and CD, CM beyond 100); no symbol is repeated more than three times in a row; V and L are never repeated. The lab builds numbers with tiles, reads numerals by splitting them into tens and ones, and asks children to judge three classmates' answers.","Common mistakes. Children add every symbol (reading IX as 11), write four in a row (XXXX for 40), invent pairs such as IL for 49 or VX for 5, or treat the letters like digits in columns. Encourage them to write the tens and the ones separately: 99 is XC and IX, never IC. Clock faces that use IIII are an old tradition, not today's rule."]},"familyTip":"Look for Roman numerals on clock faces, book chapters, film credits and the names of kings and queens, such as Henry VIII. Read each one together by splitting it into tens and ones: XLIX is XL and IX, so 40 + 9 = 49. Some clocks show 4 as IIII: ask why that breaks today's rule.","keyIdea":"Roman numerals use letters for numbers: I is 1, V is 5, X is 10, L is 50 and C is 100. Write the biggest symbols first and add them up, so XXVI is 10 + 10 + 5 + 1 = 26. The same symbol never goes more than three times in a row. Instead, a small symbol just before a bigger one is taken away. Only four of these pairs are needed up to 100: IV is 4, IX is 9, XL is 40 and XC is 90. There is no Roman numeral for zero.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it more or less than 50? Drag each numeral, or tap it and then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Less than 50","More than 50"],"items":[["XL",0],["LX",1],["XLIX",0],["LI",1],["XXXIX",0],["XC",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagroman","caption":"Flagship lab: build numbers from I, V, X, L and C tiles, take away with IV, IX, XL and XC, read numerals by splitting them, and check children's numerals.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
